WEBVTT

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:38.180
<v Ira Glass>OK, so I don't know any other way to get into this than to just say it. I'm alarmed about something with this year's election. And yes, I know everybody is alarmed about this election. That feels like the one bipartisan thing going on in our country, is that everybody on both sides is kind of a little freaked out. But even in that context, there was this one moment where I felt like, oh, this is the world that we're living in? Like, it's this bad? This is a moment that was widely reported. I bet you saw this. September 16, Donald Trump held a press conference to say that he now believes that Barack Obama was born in the United States. And then he added this claim.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我不知道除了直接说出来还有什么别的方法能切入这个话题。我对今年的选举有些担忧。是的，我知道每个人都对这次选举感到担忧。这感觉像是我们国家唯一一件两党达成共识的事，那就是双方的所有人都感到有点恐慌。但即使在那种背景下，也有一个瞬间让我觉得，哦，这就是我们生活的世界吗？真的有这么糟糕吗？这是一个被广泛报道的时刻。我敢打赌你肯定看到了。9月16日，唐纳德·特朗普召开新闻发布会，称他现在相信巴拉克·奥巴马出生在美国。然后他补充了这一主张。

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:54.090
<v Donald Trump>Hillary Clinton and her campaign of 2008 started the birther controversy. I finished it. I finished it. You know what I mean.
<v Donald Trump>希拉里·克林顿和她2008年的竞选团队开启了“出生地争议”。我结束了它。我结束了它。你知道我的意思。

00:00:54.090 --> 00:01:11.450
<v Ira Glass>Jake Tapper was doing the live coverage of the press conference on CNN. He's their chief Washington correspondent. And he knew the whole history of the birther controversy. He had covered this for years, knew it backwards and forwards. And I got in touch with him because I wondered what it was like to be on live television when Trump announced this.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·塔珀当时正在CNN直播这场新闻发布会。他是他们的首席华盛顿记者。他了解“出生地争议”的整个历史。他报道此事多年，对此了如指掌。我联系了他，因为我想知道当特朗普宣布这件事时，站在直播镜头前是什么感觉。

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:30.340
<v Jake Tapper>It was just-- it was just bizarre. And there's a famous quote from the writer Mary McCarthy who was in a feud with another writer, Lillian Hellman, and she said, "every word she says is a lie, including 'and' and 'the.'" And that's what it was, watching that press conference.
<v Jake Tapper>那简直——那简直太离奇了。作家玛丽·麦卡锡曾和另一位作家莉莲·海尔曼有过恩怨，她有一句名言：“她说出的每一个字都是谎言，包括‘和’（and）与‘的’（the）。”看着那场新闻发布会，感觉就是那样。

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.320
<v Ira Glass>In the live coverage, Tapper almost sounds mad. Here he is on TV.
<v Ira Glass>在直播报道中，塔珀听起来几乎很愤怒。这是他在电视上的表现。

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:48.150
<v Jake Tapper>Donald Trump said that Barack Obama was born in the United States, which has been a fact since 1961, but congratulations for realizing it. And then he said, Hillary Clinton started birtherism-- not true-- and I ended it-- also not true.
<v Jake Tapper>唐纳德·特朗普说巴拉克·奥巴马出生在美国，这自1961年以来就是一个事实，但恭喜他终于意识到了。然后他说，希拉里·克林顿开启了出生地怀疑论——这不是真的——而我结束了它——这也不是真的。

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:59.100
<v Ira Glass>Watching you, what it seemed like is that you were feeling something like, oh, something so wrong is happening here. A lie is being born in front of my eyes. I must stop this somehow.
<v Ira Glass>看着你，感觉你当时好像在想：哦，这里正在发生极其错误的事情。一个谎言正在我眼前诞生。我必须以某种方式阻止它。

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:24.720
<v Jake Tapper>I have no delusions that I can stop a lie. [CHUCKLES] But, I mean, I just wanted anybody watching who heard this to know that it was wrong. It was just false. He didn't end it. He perpetuated it. He made the birther lie bigger than it had ever been. But let me add one thing just because I do want to say one thing about--
<v Jake Tapper>我没有妄想自己能阻止一个谎言。（轻笑）但是，我的意思是，我只是想让每一个听到这些话的观众都知道这是错的。这纯属虚假。他并没有结束它。他延续了它。他让关于出生地的谎言变得比以往任何时候都更大。但让我补充一点，因为我确实想说一件事关于——

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:50.580
<v Ira Glass>OK. At this point, Jake Tapper could not control himself. He ran through all the facts about how we know that Hillary Clinton's campaign did not start the birther issue, and this has been summarized plenty of places by now. Look it up if you're curious. Back in that September press conference, it was the boldness of the lie that was disturbing. Like, seriously? Do you think they were going to believe you? Or do you know that we're not going to believe you and you just don't care?
<v Ira Glass>好的。此时此刻，杰克·塔珀无法控制自己了。他讲完了所有事实，说明我们如何得知希拉里·克林顿的竞选团队并没有发起“出生地争议”，这一点现在已经在很多地方被总结过了。如果你好奇，可以去查一下。在那场九月的发布会上，这种谎言的厚颜无耻令人不安。真的吗？你以为他们会相信你吗？还是说你知道我们不会相信你，但你根本不在乎？

00:02:50.580 --> 00:03:06.370
<v Ira Glass>And it didn't take long for the results of the lie to be measured. Just a few days after the Trump press conference, a Monmouth poll showed that one third of likely voters in Florida believed that Hillary Clinton started the birther controversy. I told this to Jake.
<v Ira Glass>谎言的后果很快就显现出来了。在特朗普新闻发布会几天后，一项蒙茅斯民意调查显示，三分之一的佛罗里达州潜在选民认为希拉里·克林顿开启了“出生地争议”。我把这件事告诉了杰克。

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.300
<v Jake Tapper>[SIGHS]
<v Jake Tapper>（叹气）

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.360
<v Ira Glass>Your reaction?
<v Ira Glass>你的反应？

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:17.440
<v Jake Tapper>I mean, there is a large percentage of the country that thinks Barack Obama is a Muslim.
<v Jake Tapper>我的意思是，这个国家有很大比例的人认为巴拉克·奥巴马是穆斯林。

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.810
<v Ira Glass>That would also be a third, about 30%. He's a Christian.
<v Ira Glass>那也是三分之一，大约30%。他是基督徒。

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:40.420
<v Jake Tapper>I mean, there are a lot of people who believe a lot of wrong things. It's-- it's crazy. I mean, and it's dispiriting. I mean, it's just-- I've never seen anything like this.
<v Jake Tapper>我的意思是，有很多人相信很多错误的事情。这——这太疯狂了。我的意思是，这令人沮丧。我从未见过这样的事。

00:03:40.420 --> 00:04:08.570
<v Ira Glass>In just the last two weeks Donald Trump has said that Hillary Clinton may have been on drugs in the second debate. He said Hillary Clinton, quote, "meets in secret with international banks to plot the destruction of US sovereignty." During the debates, he denied saying that climate change is a hoax created by China and that Japan should get nuclear weapons even though, A, many of us remember him saying those things. It was not that long ago. And B, we can all just find this in our phones in, like, seconds.
<v Ira Glass>就在过去两周，唐纳德·特朗普声称希拉里·克林顿在第二次辩论中可能吸了毒。他说希拉里·克林顿“私下会见国际银行，密谋破坏美国主权”。在辩论期间，他否认曾说过气候变化是中国制造的骗局，以及日本应该拥有核武器，尽管A，我们许多人都记得他说过这些话。那是没多久前的事。B，我们所有人只要在手机上花几秒钟就能查到。

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:20.310
<v Ira Glass>These lies are perplexing in their nakedness. Like, he knows lots of us know these are lies, but we're in some kind of new kind of universe where that is normal and that is OK and we will just accept that.
<v Ira Glass>这些谎言因其赤裸裸的特性而令人困惑。就像，他知道我们很多人都知道这是谎言，但我们处于一种新的宇宙中，谎言变成了常态，变成了可以接受的事，我们只能选择接受。

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:36.570
<v Jake Tapper>It's crazy. I mean, honestly, Joe McCarthy said crazy things in, you know, the '50s, but I've never seen accusations this wild in American politics from a major, major political figure.
<v Jake Tapper>这太疯狂了。说实话，约瑟夫·麦卡锡在50年代说过疯狂的话，但我从未在现代美国政治中看到一位重量级政治人物做出如此狂野的指控。

00:04:36.570 --> 00:05:03.800
<v Ira Glass>I feel worried about the country. Like, I feel worried that we live in a country where so many people distrust the fact-based media and fact-based journalists and are getting their information from other news sources and that things like this can enter the atmosphere, and then they become true for so many people. I feel worried.
<v Ira Glass>我为这个国家感到担忧。我觉得我生活在一个国家，这里有太多人不仅不信任基于事实的媒体和记者，还从其他新闻源获取信息，以至于这种事情能够进入公共氛围，并让许多人信以为真。我感到担忧。

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:37.810
<v Jake Tapper>What's uncomfortable about this election is that the Trump campaign-- not the Republican Party, but the Trump campaign-- has made it so when you stand up for facts and things that are true, they act as though those facts are partisan. And that's very uncomfortable because I do not belong to either major political party. I don't think the Democratic Party or the Republican Party have answers to the nation's problems independently.
<v Jake Tapper>这次选举让人不安的是，特朗普竞选团队——不是共和党，而是特朗普竞选团队——已经把局面搞成这样：当你站出来捍卫事实和真相时，他们就表现得好像这些事实是党派偏见一样。这非常令人不安，因为我不属于任何一个主要政党。我不认为民主党或共和党能独立解决国家的问题。

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:53.650
<v Ira Glass>In addition to his live coverage, Jake did a whole fact-check segment with FactCheck.org about whether Hillary Clinton started the birther movement. But, of course, he's on a network that is seen as so partisan-- it's so hated by some Republicans-- that stadiums full of people chant against them at Trump rallies.
<v Ira Glass>除了直播报道，杰克还和FactCheck.org合作做了一个完整的核查片段，关于希拉里·克林顿是否发起了“出生地运动”。当然，他身处一个被视为非常有党派偏见的电视网——它被一些共和党人极度憎恨——以至于在特朗普的集会上，满场的人都在高喊反对他们。

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.040
<v Crowd>CNN sucks!
<v Crowd>CNN烂透了！

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.120
<v Ira Glass>CNN sucks.
<v Ira Glass>CNN烂透了。

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:01.370
<v Crowd>CNN sucks! CNN sucks! CNN sucks! CNN sucks!
<v Crowd>CNN烂透了！CNN烂透了！CNN烂透了！CNN烂透了！

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:10.080
<v Rush Limbaugh>This fact-check technique is the latest. Let me tell you what it really is. There is no fact-checking.
<v Rush Limbaugh>这种事实核查技巧是最近的手段。让我告诉你它到底是什么。根本没有所谓的事实核查。

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.140
<v Ira Glass>This is Rush Limbaugh, a couple weeks ago.
<v Ira Glass>这是拉什·林博几周前说的话。

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:56.710
<v Rush Limbaugh>The fact that The New York Times and The Washington Post and USA Today and all these other papers and networks now have fact-checkers is for one reason. It allows them to fool you. The idea that it is a fact-check story is designed to say to you that it is objective and analytically fair. And all it is is a vehicle for them to do opinion journalism under the guise of fairness, which, if you fall for it, gives it even more power.
<v Rush Limbaugh>《纽约时报》、《华盛顿邮报》、《今日美国》以及其他所有报纸和电视网现在有核查员，原因只有一个。这让他们可以欺骗你。所谓的事实核查报道，其目的是让你觉得它是客观且分析公正的。而这实际上只是他们披着公平的外衣进行观点新闻报道的工具，如果你相信了它，反而给了它更多的力量。

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:32.760
<v Ira Glass>So that's where we are right now. The presentation of facts is seen as partisan opinion, and then every day a barrage of untruths are presented as truth, and we're just supposed to suck it up. That's the moment we live in. That's our country right now. And this is going to continue after this election, no matter who wins. Like, this is the rest of our lives, I think, this post-truth politics. With so many of us getting our news from social media and from sources that we agree with, it's easier than ever to check if a fact is true, and facts matter less than ever.
<v Ira Glass>这就是我们现在的处境。事实的呈现被视为党派观点，而每天都有大量的谎言被当作真理展示，我们只能无奈接受。这就是我们所处的时代。这就是我们现在的国家。无论谁获胜，这种情况在选举后仍会持续。我觉得，这就是我们余生要面对的“后真相政治”。随着我们中越来越多的人通过社交媒体和我们认同的来源获取新闻，检查事实真伪比以往任何时候都容易，但事实却变得比以往任何时候都无关紧要。

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:44.820
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, we take stock of how far we've fallen, and we've got some other stuff, too-- about truth and the elections. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的节目中，我们将评估我们堕落到了什么地步，我们也还有其他内容——关于真相和选举。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:07:44.820 --> 00:08:16.030
<v Ira Glass>So before I say anything else, I want to say, of course, there is solid, fact-based journalism done on the right. That is not what I'm going to be talking about right now. I'm talking about the stew of websites, talk radio, and commentary that churn out all kinds of stuff every day that is half-true or not true at all. And I feel like for months I have been thinking about this just all the time, how extreme the gap is this year between the reality put forth by the mainstream media and the reality put forth by the right-wing media. Like, why is the gap so big?
<v Ira Glass>在说其他话之前，我想先说明，当然，右翼也有扎实的、基于事实的报道。我说的不是那些。我说的是那些充斥在网站、谈话电台和评论中的内容，每天都在炮制半真半假甚至完全虚假的信息。几个月来，我一直在思考这个问题：为什么主流媒体所呈现的现实与右翼媒体所呈现的现实之间的鸿沟在今年变得如此巨大？为什么差距这么大？

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:35.260
<v Ira Glass>This was really driven home to me recently when I've been talking to my uncle Lenny on the phone. My uncle Lenny's 81. He's a retired plastic surgeon outside of San Diego. He gets all of his news from Fox and The Wall Street Journal . Not into President Obama or Hillary Clinton at all. Doesn't like Trump very much, but he's voting for him as the lesser of two evils.
<v Ira Glass>最近，当我与莱尼叔叔通电话时，这一点深深触动了我。莱尼叔叔81岁了，是一位退休的整形外科医生，住在圣地亚哥郊外。他所有的新闻都来自福克斯电视台和《华尔街日报》。他一点也不喜欢奥巴马总统或希拉里·克林顿。他不太喜欢特朗普，但他认为特朗普是两害相权取其轻，所以准备投票给他。

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:36.620
<v Uncle Lenny>Ira. Good morning.
<v Uncle Lenny>艾拉。早上好。

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:39.530
<v Ira Glass>Hey. Have you been for your walk today yet?
<v Ira Glass>嘿。你今天去散步了吗？

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:41.970
<v Uncle Lenny>No, I'll do that after we finish.
<v Uncle Lenny>没，等我们聊完再去。

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:45.320
<v Ira Glass>Really? I thought the walk was more of a first thing in the morning kind of activity.
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？我以为散步应该是早上第一件事。

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:50.000
<v Uncle Lenny>Well, it sometimes is, but I don't want to be too rigid.
<v Uncle Lenny>有时是这样，但我不想太死板。

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.130
<v Ira Glass>You don't want to be somebody who at night eats exactly seven almonds.
<v Ira Glass>你不想成为那种晚上精确吃七颗杏仁的人。

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.110
<v Uncle Lenny>[LAUGHS] That's right.
<v Uncle Lenny>（笑）没错。

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.920
<v Ira Glass>That's a crazy person.
<v Ira Glass>那是疯子。

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:24.150
<v Ira Glass>That's right, I'm telling my Republican uncle an Obama joke just to make him happy. A few weeks ago, I started recording conversations with my Uncle Lenny about the election. Mostly we read The New York Times together online and he would tell me what he thought of the stories. But what struck me most about these conversations is when Uncle Lenny would talk about Hillary Clinton, and especially about Barack Obama, the way he sees them. The story in his head about them seemed very out there.
<v Ira Glass>没错。我给我的共和党叔叔讲了一个奥巴马笑话，只是为了让他开心。几周前，我开始录制与莱尼叔叔关于选举的对话。我们大部分时间是在网上一起看《纽约时报》，他会告诉我他对这些报道的看法。但这些对话中最让我震惊的是，当莱尼叔叔谈到希拉里·克林顿，特别是巴拉克·奥巴马时，他眼中的他们。他脑海中关于他们的故事似乎非常离谱。

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:27.060
<v Ira Glass>Hey, can I ask, what do you think of Obama?
<v Ira Glass>嘿，我可以问问，你觉得奥巴马怎么样？

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:40.340
<v Uncle Lenny>I think he is an intelligent man who's never done anything worthwhile in his life. A print at--
<v Uncle Lenny>我认为他是一个聪明人，但他这辈子从来没做过什么有价值的事。印刷——

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:48.240
<v Ira Glass>Hey, wait, stop right there. I feel like I find that confusing. Like, he's the first black president. That's an incredible thing. That's an incredible thing.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，等等，停在那儿。我觉得这很令人困惑。他是第一位黑人总统。那是一件了不起的事情。那是一件了不起的事情。

00:09:48.240 --> 00:10:01.300
<v Uncle Lenny>Well, symbolically it is. But his performance has been that of an amateur, which he also is. He's done nothing. He's played more rounds of golf than any president in history.
<v Uncle Lenny>嗯，象征意义上是的。但他的表现就像个业余爱好者，他确实也是。他什么都没做。他打的高尔夫球比历史上任何一位总统都多。

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:21.420
<v Ira Glass>I checked this later. Untrue. Dwight Eisenhower played nearly three times as much golf, Woodrow Wilson played four times more than Obama. But everything in Obama's biography seems suspicious to Lenny. Obama claims to have run the Harvard Law Review, Lenny told me. Why didn't he write a single article for them? I looked into it. He did.
<v Ira Glass>我后来查过了。这是假的。德怀特·艾森豪威尔打的高尔夫球几乎是奥巴马的三倍，伍德罗·威尔逊打的次数是奥巴马的四倍。但奥巴马传记中的一切在莱尼看来似乎都很可疑。莱尼告诉我，奥巴马声称曾管理过《哈佛法律评论》。他为什么没为他们写过一篇文章？我查了一下。他写过。

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:28.910
<v Uncle Lenny>And no one in his graduating class from law school can remember ever having seen him there.
<v Uncle Lenny>而且他法学院同届没有人记得见过他。

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:31.370
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I know somebody from his law school class who knew him.
<v Ira Glass>哦，我认识一个他法学院同学，确实认识他。

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:32.390
<v Uncle Lenny>Did he know him?
<v Uncle Lenny>他认识他吗？

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:35.880
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Ayelet. My friend, Ayelet. She knew him in law school.
<v Ira Glass>是的。艾耶莱特。我的朋友，艾耶莱特。她在法学院时认识他。

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:41.000
<v Uncle Lenny>Well, that's the first person I've ever heard of who said they knew him in law school.
<v Uncle Lenny>嗯，这是我听到的第一个说在法学院认识他的人。

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:54.030
<v Ira Glass>Notice that we're not even out of law school yet, and already this is a narrative where the most mundane details about the man's life are doubted. Already there's this tone of conspiracy and cover-up over nothing.
<v Ira Glass>注意，我们甚至还没走出法学院的话题，这就已经变成了一个叙事：这个男人生命中极其平凡的细节都被怀疑。关于这名男子，已经笼罩着一种毫无根据的阴谋和掩盖的论调。

00:10:54.030 --> 00:11:23.820
<v Ira Glass>And I bring this up because these details are part of a story that grew up around Obama in the right-wing media. He's not what he seems. He has a secret agenda. This story got traction, of course, partly because he's black, but the right-wing media would probably do the same thing with a white candidate. I mean, look at some of the things they've said about Hillary Clinton, a candidate who gives them plenty of legitimate things to criticize, and yet somehow they dwell on this nonstop speculation about her health, the earpiece she supposedly wore at the debates.
<v Ira Glass>我提到这一点是因为这些细节是在右翼媒体中围绕奥巴马成长起来的故事的一部分。他不是他看起来的那样。他有一个秘密议程。当然，这个故事能产生影响，部分是因为他是黑人，但右翼媒体对于白人候选人可能也会做同样的事。我的意思是，看看他们对希拉里·克林顿说过的一些话，一个给了他们很多合法批评理由的候选人，然而他们不知为何却不停地推测她的健康状况，以及她在辩论中所谓佩戴的耳塞。

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:34.270
<v Ira Glass>When Lenny and I talked about this, the story that he believes about Barack Obama and the one that I believe based on the mainstream media, each of us was like, really? You believe that?
<v Ira Glass>当莱尼和我谈论这件事时，他相信的关于巴拉克·奥巴马的故事，和我根据主流媒体相信的故事，我们彼此都觉得：真的吗？你相信那个？

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:45.740
<v Uncle Lenny>He hates what America stands for. To him it stands for slavery, it stands for exceptionalism, it stands for mal-distribution of wealth.
<v Uncle Lenny>他憎恨美国所代表的一切。对他来说，美国代表着奴隶制，代表着例外论，代表着财富分配不均。

00:11:45.740 --> 00:12:01.140
<v Ira Glass>When Lenny talks about Obama's policies, it is all conspiracy. The president is a Muslim. He's on a mission to put Muslims in positions of power and influence. He has an ultra-left-wing agenda to erase the borders with Canada and Mexico and end the United States as we know it.
<v Ira Glass>当莱尼谈论奥巴马的政策时，全是阴谋论。总统是穆斯林。他的使命是将穆斯林安插到权力和影响力的岗位上。他有一个极左的议程，要抹除与加拿大和墨西哥的边界，终结我们所知的美国。

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:24.890
<v Uncle Lenny>This guy-- he wants to have one country of North America, which is composed of Canada, the United States, and part of Mexico, if not all of Mexico. That's why the existing laws, which dictate that border trespassers shall be deported, he chooses to ignore.
<v Uncle Lenny>这个家伙——他想建立一个北美单一国家，由加拿大、美国和部分墨西哥组成，如果不是全部墨西哥的话。这就是为什么他选择无视规定边境非法越境者应被驱逐出境的法律。

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:29.850
<v Ira Glass>Well, no, he actually deported 2.5 million people. More than any other president.
<v Ira Glass>不，他实际上驱逐了250万人。比任何其他总统都多。

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:36.020
<v Uncle Lenny>I don't believe that, Ira, for one minute. I don't believe that.
<v Uncle Lenny>我不相信，艾拉，一分钟也不信。我不相信。

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:58.570
<v Ira Glass>OK, I love my uncle. I remember crying as a kid when he went off to Vietnam. Back in the '70s and '80s, he hated liberal politicians, but he hated them because they were liberals. Like, they spent too much money, they were soft on defense. Like, that was enough. He didn't believe these kind of dark conspiracies. That's the thing that's changed, for him, and lots of people, I think.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我爱我的叔叔。我记得小时候他去越南时我曾哭过。回到70年代和80年代，他憎恨自由派政客，但他憎恨他们是因为他们是自由派。比如，他们花钱太多，国防态度软弱。比如，这就足够了。他不相信这类黑暗的阴谋。这就是变化的地方，对于他，以及我想对于很多人来说。

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:33.150
<v Ira Glass>And those numbers that I quoted him are true. They're from the Department of Homeland Security. But facts do not have a fighting chance against this right-wing fable. And in the last few weeks since I started obsessing over all the lies that are being presented as truth right now, at some point I realized, like, oh, maybe the defining issue of this election is built around a story that isn't true. Like, honestly, this is one that I just took for granted. Like, people talk about this all the time. I hadn't given it a second thought. This issue that propelled Donald Trump to win the Republican nomination. You know the one.
<v Ira Glass>而我引用给他的那些数字是真的。它们来自国土安全部。但事实在这一右翼寓言面前毫无招架之力。在过去几周，自从我开始痴迷于目前被当作真理呈现的所有谎言后，我意识到，哦，也许这场选举的核心问题建立在一个不真实的故事上。说实话，这是我一直理所当然认为的问题。人们一直在谈论这个。我没再多想。这个问题推动了唐纳德·特朗普赢得了共和党提名。你知道我说的是哪个。

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:40.320
<v Donald Trump>I will build a great, great wall on our southern border, and I will have Mexico pay for that wall.
<v Donald Trump>我会在我们的南部边境建一座伟大、伟大的墙，我会让墨西哥支付这笔费用。

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:40.700
<v Crowd>Yes!
<v Crowd>是的！

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:41.580
<v Donald Trump>Mark my words.
<v Donald Trump>记住我的话。

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:42.640
<v Crowd>Yes!
<v Crowd>是的！

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:47.030
<v Ira Glass>The premise of the wall is that immigration is out of control. Trump told an interviewer on Fox--
<v Ira Glass>筑墙的前提是移民问题失控了。特朗普在福克斯电台采访时说——

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:57.630
<v Donald Trump>I'm not just talking Mexico. I'm talking about all over the world, they're coming through the southern border. You're going to have them coming in from the Middle East. You have them coming all over. It's not just Mexico. Everybody's pouring through the border.
<v Donald Trump>我指的不只是墨西哥。我说的是全世界，他们都通过南部边境涌入。你会看到他们从中东涌入。你有来自各地的移民。不只是墨西哥。每个人都在涌过边境。

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:28.570
<v Ira Glass>So when this became the central issue of the Republican primaries, the wall, to me, seemed like it might not be the most practical solution to this problem, but I figured this was a response to a problem facing the country. People might agree or disagree in the way that they would with any policy. But if you look into it, you come right away to this fact. And this is not a big hidden secret or anything. It shows up in news stories from time to time. This flood of people pouring across the border-- the total increase in the number of illegal immigrants in the United States each year-- is zero.
<v Ira Glass>所以当这成为共和党初选的中心议题时，在我看来，这堵墙可能不是解决这个问题的最实际方案，但我认为这是对国家所面临问题的一种回应。人们可能像对待任何政策一样赞同或反对。但如果你深入研究，你马上会发现这个事实。这不是什么巨大的隐藏秘密。它不时出现在新闻报道中。这股涌过边境的人潮——美国每年非法移民总数的增长——是零。

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:34.310
<v Alex Nowrasteh>I think he's describing an alternative reality. Or at best, a reality that existed over a decade ago.
<v Alex Nowrasteh>我认为他描述的是一个替代现实。或者往好了说，是一个十多年前存在的现实。

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:42.470
<v Ira Glass>This is Alex Nowrasteh. He's a conservative, a Republican, the immigration policy analyst at the Cato Institute, a libertarian think tank.
<v Ira Glass>这是亚历克斯·诺拉斯特。他是一位保守派、共和党人，也是自由意志主义智库卡托研究所的移民政策分析师。

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:46.070
<v Ira Glass>And so there's not a flood of illegal immigrants right now.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在根本没有非法移民潮。

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:53.450
<v Alex Nowrasteh>There isn't. There are new illegal immigrants entering the country each year, but they are offset by the illegal immigrants who are leaving.
<v Alex Nowrasteh>确实没有。每年都有新的非法移民进入该国，但他们被正在离开的非法移民所抵消。

00:14:53.450 --> 00:15:20.860
<v Ira Glass>There's about 350,000 in each direction each year. And it's been that way for years, since 2009. In fact, between 2007 and 2009, at the start of the recession, when jobs dried up, the number of illegal immigrants in our country fell by about a million people. And so why do so many people believe that we're being flooded with immigrants if, in fact, we are not being flooded with immigrants? I ask that question, but of course, you know what the answer is.
<v Ira Glass>每年双向大约都有35万人。这种情况已经持续多年了，自2009年以来就是这样。事实上，在2007年到2009年经济衰退初期，当工作机会减少时，我国的非法移民数量减少了大约100万人。那么，为什么这么多人相信我们正被移民潮淹没，如果事实上我们并没有被淹没呢？我问这个问题，但当然，你知道答案是什么。

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:24.260
<v Ann Coulter>I mean, the problem is out of control. We need a fence on the border like Israel has.
<v Ann Coulter>我的意思是，问题已经失控了。我们需要像以色列那样在边境建围栏。

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:30.360
<v Sean Hannity>All right, listen. Look at the impact it's having on our health care system, our criminal justice system, our educational system.
<v Sean Hannity>好了，听着。看看它对我们的医疗系统、刑事司法系统、教育系统造成的影响。

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:52.900
<v Ira Glass>That's Ann Coulter and Sean Hannity on Fox TV two years ago. Stories about our out-of-control immigration system have been a staple of the right-wing media for years now. Ann Coulter here is talking about President Obama's immigration policy, which includes some permissive things but, like I told my Uncle Lenny, he's ordered removals of more illegal immigrants from the country than any president-- 2.5 million.
<v Ira Glass>那是两年前福克斯电视上的安·库尔特和肖恩·汉尼提。关于我们移民系统失控的报道多年来一直是右翼媒体的主打内容。安·库尔特在这里谈论的是奥巴马总统的移民政策，其中包括一些宽容的举措，但正如我告诉莱尼叔叔的，他下令驱逐的非法移民比任何总统都多——250万人。

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.860
<v Ira Glass>Coulter characterizes his policy towards immigrants this way.
<v Ira Glass>库尔特这样描述他的移民政策。

00:15:55.860 --> 00:16:14.180
<v Ann Coulter>Obama just wants-- get 'em in. Just get 'em in. And in a matter of years, they'll be voting Democrat and collecting welfare. Unfortunately, the country can't afford it. We have our own poor people we need to take care of. And we're running out of money for that. It's completely out of control.
<v Ann Coulter>奥巴马只是想——把他们弄进来。就是把他们弄进来。几年内，他们就会投票给民主党并领取福利。不幸的是，这个国家负担不起。我们有自己的穷人需要照顾。而我们的钱快花光了。这完全失控了。

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:26.650
<v Ira Glass>Researching all this-- I hadn't listened to Rush Limbaugh for a while, and listening I remembered, oh right, he just makes facts up. Like, that's standard operating procedure on right-wing talk radio.
<v Ira Glass>研究这一切时——我有一段时间没听拉什·林博的节目了，听的时候我想起来，哦对了，他就是凭空捏造事实。这就是右翼谈话电台的标准操作程序。

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:30.560
<v Ira Glass>Here he is, trying to give a little immigration policy history.
<v Ira Glass>这是他，试图提供一点移民政策历史。

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:57.870
<v Rush Limbaugh>You know, I still am amazed by the reaction I get from people when I tell them that there was zero immigration in this country from 1924 to 1965. People don't know it. It's not reported. It's never been part of history class, history education. 41 years, essentially, there was no immigration.
<v Rush Limbaugh>你知道，当人们听到我告诉他们1924年到1965年这个国家没有移民时，我依然对他们的反应感到震惊。人们不知道。它没有被报道。它从来没有成为历史课、历史教育的一部分。41年，本质上，没有移民。

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:05.730
<v Rush Limbaugh>Try telling people that in the midst of this debate, and they won't believe you. They'll think you're making it up. They'll think you're lying about it.
<v Rush Limbaugh>试着在这场辩论中告诉人们，他们不会相信你。他们会认为你在编造。他们会认为你在撒谎。

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:20.520
<v Ira Glass>Well, there's a good reason they think you're making it up and lying. Millions of people became legal immigrants during that period. 8 million were granted status as permanent residents. 6.5 million became citizens, according to the federal government.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，他们认为你在编造和撒谎是有充分理由的。在此期间，数百万的人成为了合法移民。根据联邦政府的数据，800万人获得了永久居民身份。650万人成为了公民。

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:47.720
<v Ira Glass>Alex Nowrasteh, the guy from the Cato Institute, contests all the big plot points that you hear on right-wing media about immigration. That immigrants cause more crime than native born Americans, that they are a drain on taxpayers, and, of course, that they are flooding across the border. I wonder what it was like for him to see those ideas propagated so often, so broadly, and taken so seriously by so many people. Like, to the point where a candidate is proposing a wall and then stadiums cheer.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·诺拉斯特，那个卡托研究所的人，反驳了你在右翼媒体上听到的关于移民的所有重大论点。说移民造成的犯罪比本土出生的美国人多，说他们是纳税人的负担，当然，还有他们正涌过边境。我很好奇，看着这些观点被如此频繁、广泛地传播，并被如此多人如此认真地对待，对他来说是什么感觉。比如，到了一个候选人提议筑墙，然后全场欢呼的地步。

00:17:47.720 --> 00:18:15.730
<v Alex Nowrasteh>It's incredibly depressing to see people base their policy opinions on things that just simply are not true, that are basically just myths. Like, to say otherwise, to state what the facts otherwise are, gets you labeled a traitor or gets you labeled sort of anti-American, or gets you labeled a socialist. All things which I am not. Absolutely not. So it's depressing, and it's hard to fight against.
<v Alex Nowrasteh>看到人们基于完全不真实、本质上只是神话的东西来表达政策观点，这令人极其沮丧。比如，说出相反的事实，会让你被贴上叛徒的标签，或者被贴上反美、社会主义者的标签。所有这些我都不是。绝对不是。所以这很令人沮丧，也很难对抗。

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:24.330
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. Just walk me through that. Are you in a situation often where you're trying to present these facts and people who you feel like are your natural allies don't believe you?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。只是带我梳理一下。你是否经常遇到这种情况，你试图呈现这些事实，而那些你觉得是天然盟友的人却不相信你？

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:43.670
<v Alex Nowrasteh>Frequently. Frequently. I travel across the country all the time to speak to different groups. Out West in Arizona, in California, a lot of conservative and Republican clubs. And just the quantity of skepticism for well-established facts on this topic is astounding.
<v Alex Nowrasteh>经常。经常。我经常去全国各地向不同的团体发表演讲。在美国西部，在亚利桑那州，在加利福尼亚州，有很多保守派和共和党俱乐部。仅仅是人们对这个主题上已确立的事实的怀疑程度就令人震惊。

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:50.280
<v Ira Glass>And are you often in the situation where people just think you're some sort of pinko, like you're working for Obama and Hillary Clinton?
<v Ira Glass>你是否经常处于那种人们认为你是某种赤色分子，好像你是在为奥巴马和希拉里·克林顿工作？

00:18:50.280 --> 00:19:18.760
<v Alex Nowrasteh>All the time. Increasingly, all the time. When I go on the radio people, will call in and they'll say something to the effect of, oh, well, of course you believe that. You also think we should make guns illegal. You know, not knowing that I'm personally quite a gun nut and I have the exact opposite opinion. So it's like, a lot of political assumptions go into this.
<v Alex Nowrasteh>一直都是。越来越多，一直都是。当我去电台时，人们会打来电话，他们会说类似的话：哦，好吧，你当然会相信那个。你也认为我们应该取缔枪支。你知道，他们不知道我个人其实是一个枪支狂热者，我的观点正好相反。所以就像，很多政治假设被预设进去了。

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:39.020
<v Ira Glass>Alex used this phrase that I'd never heard before-- Patriotic Correctness. It's like political correctness, but the right-wing version of that. Like, if you say that there is no flood of immigrants coming across the border, well, that is just out of bounds. That's not something you say. You are not patriotically correct. So people do not even give him a chance.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯用了这个我从未听说过的短语——“爱国正确”。这就像政治正确，但这是右翼的版本。比如，如果你说没有移民潮涌过边境，那么这就越界了。这不是你能说的话。你不符合“爱国正确”。所以人们甚至不给他机会。

00:19:39.020 --> 00:20:05.180
<v Ira Glass>I think that's because politics, at some level, is more than the sum of facts. It's an identity. You know, you're a Republican or you're a Democrat, and then there's a whole set of stories and beliefs that go with that identity. And facts do not have much power against a set of beliefs. Those of us in the fact business-- our facts are puny compared to that.
<v Ira Glass>我想这是因为政治，在某种程度上，不仅仅是事实的总和。它是一种认同。你知道，你是共和党人还是民主党人，然后有一整套与该认同相伴的故事和信仰。而事实在这一套信仰面前没什么力量。我们这些搞事实工作的——我们的事实在那种力量面前微不足道。

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:08.870
<v Ira Glass>Act two, Judges with Grudges.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，带着偏见的法官。

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:42.130
<v Ira Glass>So let's turn now, in this election year, to the story of one little referendum on one state ballot. Here's how we heard about it. A few years ago we devoted an entire episode of our program to the story of this judge in Georgia, a woman named Amanda Williams. Judge Williams had a history of doing things that it seemed like judges just should not do. For instance, she put a teenager named Lindsey Dills into solitary confinement for an indefinite period of time. Indefinite, like, until further notice. No end date. No access to a lawyer. Lindsey told me--
<v Ira Glass>那么让我们在这一选举年，转向一个州选票上一项小公投的故事。以下是我们听到它的方式。几年前，我们将节目整整一集献给了乔治亚州这位法官的故事，一位名叫阿曼达·威廉姆斯的女性。威廉姆斯法官有做一些法官似乎不应该做的事情的历史。例如，她将一名叫琳赛·迪尔斯的青少年置于无限期的单独监禁中。无限期，就是直到另行通知。没有结束日期。无法接触律师。琳赛告诉我说——

00:20:42.130 --> 00:21:06.410
<v Lindsey Dills>I cried a lot. Pretty much all the time I was crying. I was like, how is this happening? How is this ethical? Where am I? Like, am I in a foreign country? Have I killed someone that I don't know about? Like, how does what I did merit that type of treatment? But there's nothing I can do about it, because I can't even use the phone. I can't even send a letter.
<v Lindsey Dills>我哭了很多。几乎一直都在哭。我当时想，这是怎么发生的？这怎么符合道德？我在哪里？就像，我在国外吗？我杀了一个我不知道的人吗？就像，我做的事怎么值得那种对待？但我对此无能为力，因为我甚至不能用电话。我甚至不能写信。

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:31.390
<v Ira Glass>Finally, during this confinement, Lindsey tried to kill herself. We attempted to interviewed Judge Williams about this and some other issues, but she declined to comment on those issues. So while I was working on that story, an official commission in Georgia that is charged with investigating judicial ethics violations was also looking into Judge Williams. The commission later filed a 14-count ethics complaint against her. Shortly after that, Judge William stepped down from the bench.
<v Ira Glass>最后，在监禁期间，琳赛试图自杀。我们曾试图采访威廉姆斯法官关于此及其他问题，但她拒绝评论。所以在我处理那个故事时，乔治亚州一个负责调查司法道德违规的官方委员会也在调查威廉姆斯法官。该委员会后来对她提起了14项道德指控。不久之后，威廉姆斯法官辞职下台。

00:21:31.390 --> 00:22:05.990
<v Ira Glass>The commission that filed those charges-- before we did that story, I didn't even know anything like this existed, but all states have them. Sometimes they're called JQCs, for Judicial Qualifications Commissions-- that's what the one in Georgia is called-- and they are the way that we as a country have come up with for getting rid of judges who do bad things. Georgia's JQC seemed pretty impressive. They have a tiny staff, seven members, who work pro bono. They are not paid. And in the past decade, in addition to Judge Williams, over 60 judges in Georgia have stepped down after being investigated.
<v Ira Glass>那个提起指控的委员会——在我们做那个故事之前，我甚至不知道这世界上还存在这样的机构，但所有州都有。有时它们被称为JQC，即司法资格委员会——这就是乔治亚州那个委员会的名称——它们是我们作为一个国家想出来的，用来清除做坏事的法官的办法。乔治亚州的JQC看起来相当令人印象深刻。他们员工极少，七名成员，义务工作。他们没有报酬。在过去十年里，除了威廉姆斯法官，乔治亚州有60多名法官在被调查后辞职。

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:43.320
<v Ira Glass>So we did that story years ago. And then a few months ago, one of my sources in Georgia called me to say, guess what? Lawmakers in Georgia were trying to neuter the JQC, and the whole thing was going to be on the ballot this fall. And, OK, I know that state-level politics can be kind of nutty sometimes, but my question was, OK, here's an example of a government agency doing a good job. It would seem like a very low cost. Like, how can something like that happen, right? Like, why was this on the ballot? It seemed like the only people who would benefit from weakening the JQC would be bad judges. Like, that is not a big constituency. Like, who's going to vote for that in a democracy?
<v Ira Glass>所以我们几年前做了那个故事。然后几个月前，我在乔治亚州的一位消息人士打电话告诉我，猜怎么着？乔治亚州的议员正试图阉割JQC，而且整个事情今年秋天会出现在选票上。而且，好吧，我知道州级政治有时可能有点疯狂，但我的问题是，好吧，这是一个政府机构做好事的一个例子。这似乎成本很低。就像，这种事怎么可能发生，对吧？就像，为什么这个会出现在选票上？似乎削弱JQC的唯一受益者就是坏法官。那可不是一个庞大的选民群体。就像，谁会在民主国家投票支持那个？

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:58.570
<v Ira Glass>Well, one of our producers, Dave Kestenbaum, flew to Atlanta to figure out what was going on. A quick warning to podcast and internet listeners. There is some language in the story that we have un-beeped here in the internet version of our show. If you prefer a beeped version of our program, go to our website.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们的制作人之一，戴夫·凯斯滕鲍姆，飞往亚特兰大去弄清楚发生了什么。给播客和互联网听众的一个快速警告。在这个故事中，我们互联网版本节目中有些语言没有被消音。如果你更喜欢消音版本，请前往我们的网站。

00:22:58.570 --> 00:23:21.210
<v Dave Kestenbaum>To understand this referendum, you first need a little background on Georgia's JQC, and just how aggressive it's been at going after judges. More than anyone, the person who probably embodies the boldness and zeal of the mission is Richard Hyde. Hyde is a commission member. He was their paid investigator for a decade. I've heard him describe himself as "the point of the spear," and once at a legislative hearing under oath, "the turd in the punchbowl."
<v Dave Kestenbaum>要理解这次公投，你首先需要了解乔治亚州JQC的一点背景，以及它在打击法官方面是多么激进。比起任何人，最能体现其使命的果敢和热情的人可能是理查德·海德。海德是委员会成员。他当了十年的带薪调查员。我听他形容自己是“矛尖”，有一次在立法听证会上宣誓时，他自称是“潘趣酒碗里的那坨屎”。

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:26.090
<v Dave Kestenbaum>When I asked him about the 60 judges that had resigned, and that that seemed like a lot, he shrugged.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>当我问他关于那60名辞职的法官，以及这看起来是不是太多了时，他耸了耸肩。

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:32.930
<v Richard Hyde>Georgia judges are no different than judges around the country. What is different is the way we investigate judges.
<v Richard Hyde>乔治亚州的法官和全国各地的法官没什么不同。不同的是我们调查法官的方式。

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:50.300
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Hyde calls it "the Georgia method." He's traveled to Ukraine to train officials there how to do it. Basically, they'll get some tip about something a judge may have done, they'll investigate the hell out of it, and if they find something, they'll confront the judge. Hyde says he'll explain that the judge has the right to a public trial, but then he'll just say this--
<v Dave Kestenbaum>海德称之为“乔治亚方法”。他曾前往乌克兰培训那里的官员如何做。基本上，他们会得到一些关于法官可能做了某事的线索，他们会彻底调查清楚，如果发现了一些事情，他们就会与法官对质。海德说，他会解释法官有公开审判的权利，但他只会说这句话——

00:23:50.300 --> 00:24:05.850
<v Richard Hyde>Judge, given the nature of the allegations, if true, what do you think the appropriate sanction from the commission should be? And about 90 percent of the time, those judges would say, I think it is time that I leave.
<v Richard Hyde>法官，鉴于指控的性质，如果属实，你认为委员会应该给予什么适当的制裁？而大约90%的情况下，那些法官会说，我想是我该离开的时候了。

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:22.680
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Honestly, I can't tell if that question is some high-minded way of trying to engage a judge's inner sense of duty, or just a fancy version of, we can do this the easy way or the hard way.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>老实说，我看不出这个问题是一种试图唤醒法官内心责任感的高尚方式，还是仅仅是“我们可以轻松处理，也可以强硬处理”的一种华丽说法。

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:54.200
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Judges are powerful people, but Hyde is not easily intimidated. He used to be a street cop. Back then he would tow the cars of state lawmakers who had double-parked near the state capitol. Once, he shot an escaped chimpanzee who was attacking people on a street. He felt bad about that. In the Atlanta school testing scandal where teachers were doctoring students' test scores, it was Hyde who got an elementary school teacher to wear a wire, which broke the whole thing open. One news story quoted someone saying they would "rather have a wasting disease than to be investigated by Hyde." He's just so relentless.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>法官是权力人物，但海德并不容易被吓倒。他曾经是一名街头警察。那时，他会拖走那些在州议会大厦附近违规停车的州议员的车。有一次，他射杀了一只正在街上攻击人的逃脱黑猩猩。他对此感到很难过。在亚特兰大学校测试丑闻中，教师篡改学生测试分数，是海德让一名小学教师戴上窃听器，从而揭开了整个事情。一篇新闻报道引用某人的话说，他们“宁愿得消耗性疾病，也不愿被海德调查”。他就是如此无情。

00:24:54.200 --> 00:25:18.990
<v Dave Kestenbaum>But Hyde says the JQC is not out to get judges. They're just trying to protect the public. If a judge slips up, they try to work things out. Here's an example of that. When I went to interview Hyde, he'd brought along a transcript of a case they'd had to look into. It's a murder case, and the defendant is refusing to use a lawyer. Just refusing. He starts actually swearing at the judge, using profanity. The judge tries to reason with him, and then this happens. I had Hyde read this part.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>但海德说，JQC不是要整法官。他们只是试图保护公众。如果法官犯了错，他们会尝试协调解决。这里有一个例子。当我去采访海德时，他随身带了一份他们必须调查的案件的记录。这是一个谋杀案，被告拒绝使用律师。就是拒绝。他开始实际上咒骂法官，使用亵渎语言。法官试图与他讲道理，然后发生了这件事。我让海德读了这一部分。

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:20.970
<v Richard Hyde>The judge-- listen to me.
<v Richard Hyde>法官——听我说。

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:22.980
<v Richard Hyde>The defendant-- fuck you.
<v Richard Hyde>被告——去你妈的。

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:24.140
<v Richard Hyde>Listen to me.
<v Richard Hyde>法官——听我说。

