<v Richard Hyde>The defendant-- your mama.
<v Richard Hyde>被告——你妈。

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:54.930
<v Richard Hyde>The judge-- 10 years.
<v Richard Hyde>法官——10年。

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:57.460
<v Richard Hyde>The defendant-- suck my dick.
<v Richard Hyde>被告——舔我的鸡巴。

00:25:57.460 --> 00:26:02.430
<v Richard Hyde>The judge-- you know something? This is going to be an interesting trial.
<v Richard Hyde>法官——你知道吗？这将是一场有趣的审判。

00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:31.910
<v Dave Kestenbaum>The defendant threatens to kill the judge's family, and the judge just kind of loses it. When the defendant says he's going to masturbate in court, the judge says, why don't you do that right now? Do it now. Do it now. Hyde says the judge had a stellar record before this, so the JQC, when it heard about it, did what it often does. It had the judge in for a little talk, to discuss what had happened and how to avoid it again. They publicly reprimanded him and the judge went back to being a judge.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>被告威胁要杀死法官的家人，而法官只是有点失控了。当被告说要在法庭上手淫时，法官说，你为什么不现在就做？现在做。现在做。海德说，这位法官在此之前记录非常出色，所以JQC在听说这件事后，做了它经常做的事。它让这位法官来进行了一次小谈话，讨论发生了什么以及如何再次避免它。他们公开谴责了他，法官回去继续做他的法官。

00:26:31.910 --> 00:27:01.990
<v Dave Kestenbaum>But Hyde says there are more extreme cases. Like the time a judge, in court, took a gun out and told the witness she was, quote, "killing her case and might as well shoot her lawyer." And then there was this other time. An attorney said that a judge hearing her case had sexually harassed her, at one point leaving her a phone message in which he, the judge, identified himself as Santa Claus and said the following, "hey, hey, hey, little girl. Want to sit on Santa's face?" The woman gave the recording of that message to Richard Hyde, and Hyde went out to talk to the judge.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>但海德说还有更极端的案件。比如有一次，一名法官在法庭上拔出一把枪，告诉证人说她在，引用，“毁掉她的案子，还不如开枪打死她的律师”。还有另一次。一名律师说，审理她案件的法官曾对她进行性骚扰，有一次给她留了一条电话留言，他，法官，自称是圣诞老人并说了以下内容，“嘿，嘿，嘿，小女孩。想坐在圣诞老人的脸上吗？”这名女子把那条留言的录音给了理查德·海德，海德去找了那位法官。

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:43.940
<v Richard Hyde>I met with him at his courthouse. And he's got an office full of people doing whatever it was that was going on, and I identified myself and asked him if we could talk in private. So we walked outside, and I'm asking him questions about, have you ever sexually harassed any of the women in front of you? Did you ever make any sexual demands? Did you ever leave a message on somebody's voicemail inappropriately? He denied all of that. So I said, well, then we have a problem because somebody is lying to me. Either the complainant or you are lying.
<v Richard Hyde>我在他的法院见了他。他的办公室里有一群人在做着不管是什么的事情，我表明了身份，问我们是否可以私下谈谈。所以我们走到外面，我问他问题，有没有对你面前的任何女性进行过性骚扰？有没有提出过任何性要求？有没有在某人的语音信箱上留过不适当的留言？他否认了所有这些。所以我说，好吧，那我们有问题了，因为有人在对我撒谎。要么是投诉人，要么是你撒谎。

00:27:43.940 --> 00:27:52.230
<v Richard Hyde>So I played-- we sat in my truck, and I played the recording, and he listened to it.
<v Richard Hyde>所以我就播放——我们坐在我的卡车里，我播放了录音，他听了。

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:56.850
<v Dave Kestenbaum>What was his reaction when you played him the voicemail? He just told you he didn't do it.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>当你播放那个语音留言时他的反应是什么？他刚才还告诉你他没做过。

00:27:56.850 --> 00:28:15.350
<v Richard Hyde>He became very emotional. He wept. He walked in his office, got a piece of stationery out and hand-wrote a letter to the governor, resigning and agreeing never to be a judge again.
<v Richard Hyde>他变得非常激动。他哭了。他走进办公室，拿出一张信纸，手写了一封给州长的信，辞职并同意永远不再做法官。

00:28:15.350 --> 00:28:54.970
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Before we get to why some in the legislature wanted to dismantle and remake the JQC, it's interesting to see how badly they wanted to change it. This is not an easy thing to do. In Georgia, like in a lot of states, the Judicial Qualifications Commission, this entity with the power to remove judges for unethical behavior, is written into the Constitution. One lawyer who helped put it there-- this was back in the '70s-- told me it was put in the Constitution expressly to avoid political tampering. People make a basic separation of powers argument. Keep the JQC independent. If lawmakers got their hands on it, they might pressure JQC members to drop cases against their friends, or go after a judge they don't like.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>在我们讨论为什么立法机构中的一些人想要拆除并重组JQC之前，看看他们是多么迫切地想要改变它是很有趣的。这不是一件容易的事。在乔治亚州，像在许多州一样，司法资格委员会，这个有权移除不道德行为法官的实体，被写进了宪法。一位帮助把它写进去的律师——那是70年代的事了——告诉我，把它放进宪法是为了明确避免政治篡改。人们提出了一个基本的权力分立论点。保持JQC的独立性。如果立法者插手，他们可能会向JQC成员施压，要求放弃针对他们朋友的案件，或者去针对他们不喜欢的法官。

00:28:54.970 --> 00:29:09.260
<v Dave Kestenbaum>But this past January, one Friday afternoon, House Resolution 1113 was introduced, a bill proposing an amendment to the Constitution that would abolish the JQC and allow lawmakers to remake it however they saw fit.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>但今年1月，一个星期五下午，众议院第1113号决议被提出，该法案提议修改宪法，废除JQC并允许立法者以他们认为合适的任何方式重组它。

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:22.610
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Lester Tate, at the time a member of the JQC, says he'd pulled into his driveway when someone called with the news. Tate was furious. It seemed like a straight-up power grab. He told me he saw HR 1113 as an act of war.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>莱斯特·泰特，当时是JQC的成员，说有人打电话告诉他这个消息时，他刚把车开进车道。泰特非常愤怒。这看起来是一场直截了当的夺权。他告诉我，他把HR 1113视为战争行为。

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:39.870
<v Lester Tate>Clearly it was. You know, when the Japanese bombed Pearl Harbor, we had no knowledge that it was coming. Nothing's been said about this to anybody, and then all of a sudden you introduce a bill like this, I think it's pretty clear that a sneak attack has been launched.
<v Lester Tate>这显然就是。你知道，当日本人轰炸珍珠港时，我们一点都不知道它要来了。没有人对任何人说过什么，然后突然间你就提出了这样的法案，我认为很明显这是一次偷袭。

00:29:39.870 --> 00:29:41.710
<v Dave Kestenbaum>It's not quite as bad as Pearl Harbor.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这还没像珍珠港那么糟。

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:47.750
<v Lester Tate>Well, no ships were sunk in the process, but the analogy, I think, still holds true.
<v Lester Tate>嗯，不，过程中没有船沉没，但我认为这个比喻依然适用。

00:29:47.750 --> 00:30:13.080
<v Dave Kestenbaum>There's one little detail worth pointing out here. It gets mentioned in a lot of the local news coverage. One of the sponsors of the bill is a representative named Johnny Caldwell. Before being a lawmaker, Caldwell was a judge. The judge who was accused of leaving the Santa Claus "sit on my face" voicemail. Representative Caldwell declined our request for an interview.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这里有一个值得指出的小细节。它在很多地方新闻报道中被提及。该法案的发起人之一是一位名叫约翰尼·考德威尔的议员。在成为立法者之前，考德威尔曾是一名法官。那位被指控留下圣诞老人“坐在我脸上”语音留言的法官。考德威尔议员拒绝了我们的采访请求。

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:17.990
<v Man>Secretary will read HR 1113.
<v Man>书记员将宣读HR 1113。

00:30:17.990 --> 00:30:20.100
<v Woman>House Resolution 1113, by Representative--
<v Woman>众议院第1113号决议，由议员——

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:38.330
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Altering the Constitution is a big deal. For starters, any proposed change has to be approved by 2/3 of the House and 2/3 of the Senate. Then the voters have to approve it as well. Republicans introduced the measure, and they had the votes in the House. But in the Senate, it was tight. If all the Republicans voted for it, the bill would pass.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>修改宪法是一件大事。首先，任何提议的变更必须获得众议院2/3和参议院2/3的批准。然后选民也必须批准它。共和党人提出了这项措施，他们在众议院拥有票数。但在参议院，情况很紧张。如果所有共和党人都投票支持，法案就会通过。

00:30:38.330 --> 00:30:58.890
<v Dave Kestenbaum>But here they had a problem. A man named Josh McKoon. McKoon is a Republican who seems to enjoy not voting Republican sometimes. He's loved politics since he was a kid. Favorite President, Calvin Coolidge. McKoon is chair of the Senate Judiciary Committee, and he thought taking the JQC out of the Constitution, letting politicians remake it however they wanted, sounded like a terrible idea.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>但在这里他们遇到了一个问题。一个名叫乔希·麦昆的人。麦昆是一位似乎有时喜欢不投票支持共和党的共和党人。他从小就热爱政治。最喜欢的总统是卡尔文·柯立芝。麦昆是参议院司法委员会主席，他认为把JQC从宪法中拿出来，让政客们随心所欲地重组，听起来是个可怕的主意。

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:06.940
<v Josh Mckoon>And I just remember somebody mentioning, hey, this is coming up. And I kind of dismissed it because it seemed so absurd.
<v Josh Mckoon>我只记得有人提到，嘿，这事要来了。我有点不理会它，因为它看起来太荒谬了。

00:31:06.940 --> 00:31:09.650
<v Man>Chair recognizes Senator 29.
<v Man>主席认可第29号参议员。

00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:10.790
<v Josh Mckoon>Thank you, Mr. President.
<v Josh Mckoon>谢谢，主席先生。

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:22.770
<v Dave Kestenbaum>This is McKoon on one of the last nights of the legislative session for the year. The bill, at this point, has failed to pass once, but it's coming up for another vote. McKoon doesn't shout. His tones is more, (INCREDULOUS) seriously?
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这是麦昆在今年立法会议最后几个晚上之一的表现。该法案此时已经失败过一次，但它即将进行另一次投票。麦昆没有大喊大叫。他的语调更多是（不可置信）真的吗？

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:46.870
<v Josh Mckoon>Um, it's just really hard to wrap my brain around the fact that we are going to do this. We ought to think long and hard before we say we're going to abolish the independent constitutional agency that has overseen judicial ethics in this state for the last four decades.
<v Josh Mckoon>嗯，真的很难想象这样一个事实：我们要这样做。在我们说要废除在过去四十年里一直监督该州司法道德的独立宪法机构之前，我们应该深思熟虑。

00:31:46.870 --> 00:32:01.220
<v Dave Kestenbaum>McKoon is particularly upset about how the legislature would remake the JQC. The JQC has seven members, and lawmakers want to give the Speaker of the House and the president of the Senate the power to pick four of the seven seats. Currently they pick zero.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>麦昆对立法机构将如何重组JQC感到特别不安。JQC有七名成员，立法者想给予众议院议长和参议院主席权力去挑选七个席位中的四个。目前他们挑选零个。

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:09.850
<v Dave Kestenbaum>I looked into what other states do. According to the Center for Judicial Ethics, only one state gives lawmakers the power to pick a majority of its JQC members.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我研究了其他州的做法。根据司法道德中心，只有一个州给予立法者挑选其JQC成员多数的权力。

00:32:09.850 --> 00:32:25.500
<v Josh Mckoon>We are taking, literally, the only bright spot in the network of ethics and conflict of interest laws in state government and we are throwing them in the garbage. And for what?
<v Josh Mckoon>我们实际上正在采取州政府道德和利益冲突法律网络中唯一的亮点，并把它们扔进垃圾桶。为了什么？

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:47.660
<v Dave Kestenbaum>McKoon began to suspect that this was all about personal gripes, or lawmakers who are friends with judges who the JQC had investigated. The Speaker of the House, in particular, seemed unusually determined to overhaul this little commission. This became very clear in the final days of the session. Time was running out to pass the bill. McKoon says he and the other Republican senators got pulled into this urgent meeting.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>麦昆开始怀疑这全是关于个人不满，或者是与JQC调查过的法官有交情的立法者。众议院议长尤其显得异常坚决要彻底整改这个小委员会。这在会议的最后几天变得非常清楚。通过该法案的时间所剩无几。麦昆说，他和共和党参议员被叫去参加了这个紧急会议。

00:32:47.660 --> 00:33:01.750
<v Josh Mckoon>We were told that the Speaker had made it clear, if we did not pass the JQC constitutional amendment, that he would, quote, "shut the building down." Meaning, the House would not consider any Senate legislation.
<v Josh Mckoon>我们被告知议长已经明确表示，如果我们不通过JQC宪法修正案，他就会，引用，“关闭大楼”。这意味着，众议院将不审议任何参议院立法。

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:03.370
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Until the JQC got passed.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>直到JQC获得通过。

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:05.730
<v Josh Mckoon>Correct.
<v Josh Mckoon>正确。

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:10.760
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Two days before the end of the session-- it's after 9:00 PM-- the bill comes up for a final vote.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>会议结束前两天——时间已过晚上9点——该法案进行了最终投票。

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:15.070
<v Man>--is agreed to. All those in favor, vote aye. Those opposed, no. The Secretary will unlock the machine.
<v Man>——已达成一致。赞成者，投票赞成。反对者，否。书记员将解锁机器。

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:16.780
<v Man>[BELL RINGS]
<v Man>（铃响）

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:54.230
<v Dave Kestenbaum>A minute later, the bill narrowly passes. If you look at the breakdown, you can see what happened. One Democrat crossed party lines and voted in favor. A woman named Donzella James. She declined to talk with us, but a newspaper reporter who was in the room, Greg Bluestein with The Atlanta Journal-Constitution , says it was totally clear what had happened. Republicans had made a trade. They'd offered to support a bill that she'd been trying to get through for years, to turn the area where she lived into a city. The bill was to put a referendum on the ballot about it. Bluestein says he knows this trade happened because a Republican, at this point, walked up and just started narrating what was going on.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>一分钟后，法案以微弱优势通过。如果你看看细分，你可以看到发生了什么。一位民主党人越过党派路线投票支持。一位名叫唐泽拉·詹姆斯的女士。她拒绝与我们交谈，但在场的报纸记者，来自《亚特兰大宪法报》的格雷格·布鲁斯坦，说发生了什么完全清楚。共和党人做了一个交易。他们主动提出支持她多年来一直试图通过的一项法案，将她居住的地区变成一个城市。该法案是为了就此在选票上进行公投。布鲁斯坦说，他知道这个交易发生了，因为一个共和党人在此时走过来并开始叙述正在发生的事情。

00:33:54.230 --> 00:34:03.480
<v Greg Bluestein>He said, she stuck with us, now we'll stick with her. So this was-- this was-- there was no effort to hide that there was horse trading going on here.
<v Greg Bluestein>他说，她坚持我们，现在我们要坚持她。所以这就是——这就是——完全没有试图掩盖这里正在进行的马匹交易。

00:34:03.480 --> 00:34:04.760
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Like, you watched it happen.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>就像，你看着它发生。

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:09.450
<v Greg Bluestein>Oh, literally watched it happen. And had people openly talk about it.
<v Greg Bluestein>哦，字面意义上看着它发生。而且有人公开谈论它。

00:34:09.450 --> 00:34:26.530
<v Dave Kestenbaum>In case there was any doubt, the very next bill brought up was for the city referendum, which passed easily. Lawmakers were willing to create a city to get control of the JQC.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>万一还有什么疑问，随后提出的下一个法案就是关于城市公投的，它轻易通过了。立法者为了获得对JQC的控制权，愿意创造一个城市。

00:34:26.530 --> 00:34:35.630
<v Dave Kestenbaum>If you're wondering why this was such a priority, why the Speaker of the House seemed so determined to push this through, Josh McKoon, the Republican, wondered that too. So we asked around.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>如果你想知道为什么这成为如此优先的事项，为什么众议院议长似乎如此决心推动它，共和党人乔希·麦昆也感到好奇。所以我们四处打听。

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:52.740
<v Josh Mckoon>You know, why is this such a high-profile-- why is this thing moving so quickly? And the answer was, this is a priority of the Speaker of the House. And then when you would say, well, why does he care about the function in the JQC? It was, well, this all goes back to his conflict with the state bar.
<v Josh Mckoon>你知道，为什么这是一个如此高调——为什么这件事发展得如此之快？答案是，这是众议院议长的优先事项。然后当你问，嗯，他为什么关心JQC的功能？答案是，这全是关于他与州律师协会的冲突。

00:34:52.740 --> 00:35:13.760
<v Dave Kestenbaum>The state bar. This takes a little explaining, but stick with me. The speaker has a longstanding, very heated feud with the state bar. The bar has filed charges against him that could get him disbarred. I've read some of the back and forth. It sounds like people yelling at each other in the most formal language possible. Like, "it is clear that absurdity is no prohibition on the actions of the state bar."
<v Dave Kestenbaum>州律师协会。这需要一点解释，但请坚持听我讲。议长与州律师协会有着长期的、非常激烈的恩怨。律师协会对他提起了可能导致他被除名的指控。我读过一些来回的往来。听起来就像人们在用尽可能正式的语言互相大喊大叫。比如，“很明显，荒谬并不能阻止州律师协会的行动。”

00:35:13.760 --> 00:35:29.920
<v Dave Kestenbaum>What does all this have to do with the JQC? The bar currently gets to a appoint three members of the JQC. If these changes go through, the bar will get zero. To McKoon, it looked like revenge. The House Speaker declined to comment.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这一切与JQC有什么关系？律师协会目前有权任命三名JQC成员。如果这些变更通过，律师协会将获得零个。对麦昆来说，这看起来像是报复。众议院议长拒绝评论。

00:35:29.920 --> 00:36:00.570
<v Dave Kestenbaum>This is the story I initially heard, at least, about how petty politics, personal gripes, and a Santa Claus voicemail were threatening to gut an institution that was doing its job, and doing it quite well. It's the kind of story that one side of an issue tells itself all the time, I think. And when you hear something like that, it can be hard to imagine any other version. But, of course, there is another side. And on that side, another story involving the very same events. So come with me. Let's cross over to that other side. Let's try to get to the bottom of this one ballot measure in one state.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这至少是我最初听到的关于琐碎的政治、个人不满和圣诞老人语音留言如何威胁到一个正在履行其职责并且做得非常好的机构的故事。我认为，这是一方在处理某个议题时经常告诉自己的故事。当你听到这样的事情时，很难想象还有其他版本。但当然，还有另一面。在那一面，有另一个涉及同样事件的故事。所以跟我来。让我们跨越到那一面。让我们试着弄清楚这一州公投的真相。

00:36:00.570 --> 00:36:14.410
<v Cynthia Becker>I am here so that the JQC never again abuses its power to the detriment of those who serve and the public we all serve.
<v Cynthia Becker>我在这里是为了让JQC不再滥用其权力去损害那些服务的人和我们共同服务的公众。

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:24.150
<v Dave Kestenbaum>This is Cynthia Becker testifying at a recent hearing before lawmakers. Until recently, she was a judge. And in the alternate narrative of this story, she's the main character.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这是辛西娅·贝克在最近一次立法者听证会上的证词。直到最近，她还是一名法官。在这个故事的替代叙事中，她是主角。

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:41.030
<v Cynthia Becker>I heard with dismay a former director testify last week, between 2010 and 2014, 50 judges were removed. We are not trophies. We are not scalps. That's not a good start.
<v Cynthia Becker>我上周惊恐地听到一名前主管作证说，2010年至2014年间，50名法官被免职。我们不是战利品。我们不是头皮。那不是一个好的开始。

00:36:41.030 --> 00:36:59.230
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Becker was investigated by the JQC and was visibly furious about how it all went down. She says she was called in to talk to JQC members about some complaint, but she wasn't told what it was. Turns out, there was a question about whether she had denied somebody bail-- a former school superintendent who ended up spending four days in jail.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>贝克被JQC调查，并对这一切如何发生感到明显愤怒。她说她被叫去与JQC成员谈论一些投诉，但她没被告知是什么。事实证明，有人质疑她是否拒绝了某人的保释——一名前学校主管，最终在监狱里待了四天。

00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:02.700
<v Dave Kestenbaum>She says when she got to the JQC one of the members just started yelling at her.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>她说当她到达JQC时，其中一名成员就开始对她大喊大叫。

00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:20.160
<v Cynthia Becker>One commission member was so over the top that, if he had conducted himself that way in a court room, deputies would have escorted him out of the courtroom. He was standing, he was red-faced, he was pointing his finger at me, lecturing me--
<v Cynthia Becker>一位委员会成员非常过分，如果他在法庭上表现成那样，法警会把他带出法庭。他站着，满脸通红，指着我，教训我——

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:21.020
<v Man>Who was that?
<v Man>那是谁？

00:37:21.020 --> 00:37:22.800
<v Cynthia Becker>That was Mr. Tate.
<v Cynthia Becker>那是泰特先生。

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:35.010
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Lester Tate, the commission member you heard comparing the JQC bill to the attack on Pearl Harbor. According to notes from that meeting, at one point, Judge Becker started weeping. The whole thing, she says, was recorded without her knowledge.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>莱斯特·泰特，你刚才听到他把JQC法案比作珍珠港事件的那位委员会成员。根据那次会议的记录，有一次，贝克法官开始哭泣。她说，整个过程是在她不知情的情况下被录音的。

00:37:35.010 --> 00:38:08.590
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Another judge who testified said that the same JQC member, Lester Tate, behaved improperly in his court. According to an affidavit by a court clerk Tate, quote, "turned bright red all over and got extremely upset." A month later, Richard Hyde, the crusading investigator you heard at the beginning of the story, showed up demanding to see documents. A court clerk wrote, quote, "he was questioning me like a drill sergeant in the army. He almost brought me to tears. I have never had anyone treat me like this." The judge, Mitchell Scoggins, says Hyde then went through his things.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>另一位作证的法官说，同一位JQC成员莱斯特·泰特在法庭上表现不当。根据法庭书记员泰特的宣誓书，引用，“全身变得通红，变得非常不安。”一个月后，你在故事开头听到的那位十字军调查员理查德·海德，出现要求查看文件。一位法庭书记员写道，引用，“他审问我像军队里的军士长。他几乎让我流泪。我从未让人这样对待过我。”米切尔·斯考金斯法官说，海德随后翻看了他的东西。

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:24.310
<v Mitchell Scoggins>the reason I know he did is because we pulled our security camera video, and we could see Mr. Hyde in there with my clerk-- just those two people-- going through paperwork on my desk. And I didn't appreciate that. I didn't like that at all.
<v Mitchell Scoggins>我知道他这样做的原因是，因为我们调取了我们的安全摄像头视频，我们可以看到海德先生和我的书记员在那里——只有他们两个人——翻看我桌上的文件。我对此不感激。我一点也不喜欢那样。

00:38:24.310 --> 00:38:31.430
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Lester Tate denies doing anything wrong in either case. In fact, he's resigned in protest over this whole effort to remake the JQC.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>莱斯特·泰特否认在任何一个案件中做错任何事。事实上，他已经辞职抗议整个重组JQC的努力。

00:38:31.430 --> 00:38:38.010
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Richard Hyde told me things did get heated when he came by court that day, but here's what he said about searching that judge's desk.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>理查德·海德告诉我，那天他去法院时事情确实变得激烈了，但关于搜查法官桌子，他是这么说的——

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:55.320
<v Richard Hyde>I looked through the papers on the public bench, yes. That is not his bench. That bench belongs to the people of this state. Those documents belong to the people of this state. They were in plain view, they looked out of place, and I looked through them. I make no apologies for that.
<v Richard Hyde>我确实翻看了公共席上的文件。那不是他的席位。那席位属于这个州的人民。那些文件属于这个州的人民。它们放在显眼的地方，它们看起来格格不入，所以我翻看了它们。我不为此道歉。

00:38:55.320 --> 00:39:09.570
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Richard Hyde has publicly said that the JQC owes a couple judges an apology for how their cases were handled-- but just a couple. Which of those judges who step down do you really want back on the bench, he asks? Most of the complaints, he says, are just from a few people.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>理查德·海德公开表示，JQC确实欠几位法官一个道歉，因为他们的案件处理方式——但仅限几位。你真的希望那些辞职的法官中哪位回到法官席上吗？他问。他说，大部分投诉仅仅来自少数人。

00:39:09.570 --> 00:39:15.080
<v Richard Hyde>Those are the five empty beer cans in the back of a pickup truck going down a dirt road.
<v Richard Hyde>那些是皮卡车后座里五个空啤酒罐，在泥路上颠簸。

00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:26.140
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Hyde was quick to say that the vast majority of judges do a fine job, under difficult circumstances. But, he says, sitting up there in that black robe with the gavel, it can go to some people's heads.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>海德很快表示，绝大多数法官在困难环境下工作出色。但是，他说，坐在那穿黑袍拿木槌的地方，它会让一些人冲昏头脑。

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:43.860
<v Richard Hyde>People stand up when they walk in the room, they stand up when they leave. They bring them coffee, they laugh at their dumb jokes. They tell them how smart, thin, tall, and funny they are. It's black robe syndrome, is what I call it.
<v Richard Hyde>人们在他们走进房间时站起来，在他们离开时站起来。他们给他们拿咖啡，他们对他们的愚蠢笑话大笑。他们告诉他们他们是多么聪明、瘦、高和有趣。这就是我所称的黑袍综合症。

00:39:43.860 --> 00:40:00.540
<v Dave Kestenbaum>One way to summarize the criticism of the JQC is that some of its members have fallen prey to their own version of black robe syndrome. After all, there is the power of being a judge. But then there is the power of judging the judges.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>总结对JQC的批评的一种方式是，它的一些成员成了他们自己版本的黑袍综合症的受害者。毕竟，拥有法官的权力是一种权力。但评判法官的权力则是另一种权力。

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:19.040
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Even after hearing these stories, it wasn't clear to me why lawmakers felt the need to amend the Constitution and give themselves control over the JQC. I didn't see how that would fix whatever problems there were. As I've said, the Speaker of the House wouldn't talk. Neither would the representative who allegedly left the Santa Claus voicemail. So I found someone else.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>即使在听了这些故事后，我还是不清楚为什么立法者觉得有必要修改宪法并给自己对JQC的控制权。我没看出那将如何修复存在的任何问题。如我所说，众议院议长不愿交谈。那位据称留下圣诞老人语音留言的议员也不愿谈。所以我找到了另一个人。

00:40:19.040 --> 00:40:52.880
<v Wendall Willard>I'm Wendall Willard. I'm a state representative, state of Georgia.
<v Wendall Willard>我是温德尔·威拉德。我是乔治亚州州众议员。

00:40:52.880 --> 00:41:04.090
<v Wendall Willard>Next thing we're reading about is there's an indictment being taken out against Judge Williams. I said, wow, she must have really done something pretty drastic to have a felony indictment.
<v Wendall Willard>接下来我们要读到的是对威廉姆斯法官的起诉书。我说，哇，她一定做了什么非常激烈的事情才有重罪起诉。

00:41:04.090 --> 00:41:11.400
<v Dave Kestenbaum>I looked this up. What she'd done, according to the indictment, was lie to the JQC. It's a felony offense with possible jail time.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我查了一下。根据起诉书，她所做的是对JQC撒谎。这是一种重罪，可能面临监禁。

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:16.110
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Is your position basically like, maybe it was correct that she stepped down for what she did, but the idea that she would--
<v Dave Kestenbaum>你的立场基本上是，也许她因为所做的事辞职是正确的，但她会因为——的想法——

00:41:16.110 --> 00:41:21.560
<v Wendall Willard>I'm not concerned about her stepping down. It's the process-- what I call the due process--
<v Wendall Willard>我不关心她辞职。这是流程——我所谓的正当程序——

00:41:21.560 --> 00:41:38.800
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Willard says these judges think they're coming in for an informal conversation, so it seems unfair to him that they can get charged with a felony if they misremember or mis-state something. He says the whole point of these meetings is to try to work things out. Most judges just need a little counseling, like the judge who imploded under a barrage of F-bombs.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>威拉德说，这些法官认为他们是来参加非正式谈话的，所以如果他们记错了或说错了一些事情就可能被控重罪，这对他来说显得不公平。他说这些会议的全部意义在于尝试协调解决。大多数法官只需要一点建议，就像那位在连珠炮般的粗话中崩溃的法官。

00:41:38.800 --> 00:42:00.630
<v Dave Kestenbaum>If every judge who goes to talk to the JQC has to worry about being charged with a felony, what judge would ever come talk to them? He says the same thing that happened to Amanda Williams also happen to Judge Becker, the one who said she was yelled at and recorded without her knowledge. She later faced six felony charges for making false statements in that meeting. The charges were dropped, but in the end, she had to promise not to be a judge again.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>如果每个去和JQC谈话的法官都必须担心被指控重罪，还有哪个法官会去和他们谈话？他说，发生在阿曼达·威廉姆斯身上的同样的事情也发生在贝克法官身上，就是那位说她在不知情的情况下被大喊大叫并被录音的法官。她后来在会议上因作虚假陈述面临六项重罪指控。指控被撤销了，但最终她不得不承诺不再做法官。

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:08.250
<v Dave Kestenbaum>The JQC has changed its policy since then. It no longer records its meetings with judges. But Becker said she felt sucker-punched by the whole thing.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>JQC自那时起改变了政策。它不再录音与法官的会议。但贝克说她觉得被整个事情暗算了一把。

00:42:08.250 --> 00:42:12.620
<v Wendall Willard>That is one segment of the concern that was growing.
<v Wendall Willard>那是担忧不断增长的一个片段。

00:42:12.620 --> 00:42:15.740
<v Dave Kestenbaum>Is that reason enough to, like, dismantle the whole JQ--
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这是拆除整个JQC的充分理由吗——

00:42:15.740 --> 00:42:32.530
<v Wendall Willard>I didn't say that was the reason alone, did I? Did I? We're not trying-- it's not a dismantling. I keep hearing that term used by the press. It is a reconstituting of the JQC. And to do so is going to require a constitutional amendment.
<v Wendall Willard>我没说那是唯一的原因，我说了吗？我说过吗？我们不是在尝试——这不是拆除。我一直听到新闻界使用这个词。这是对JQC的重组。要这样做需要宪法修正案。

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:41.440
<v Dave Kestenbaum>I asked Willard if I could just run down the things his opponents say is happening here, to play the other side's narrative for him. Actually, I had a whole list written out on a sheet of paper.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我问威拉德我是否可以把反对者说正在这里发生的事情梳理一遍，为他演示另一方的叙事。实际上，我写了一整张纸。

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:52.580
