00:44:01.710 --> 00:44:29.180
<v Dave Kestenbaum>I can see how Willard sees things. He thinks the JQC needs bigger fixes. And he figures, who better to fix it than us lawmakers? People voted us into office. We are the people's representatives. And voters will have the final say. This is going to be on the ballot next month, so people will have to decide which of these stories makes more sense to them. Or maybe they'll just hear one of the stories. That will make the decision easier. Frankly, I can't imagine most voters even know what the JQC is.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>我能看出威拉德是怎么看待事情的。他认为JQC需要更大的修复。他想，还有谁比我们立法者更适合修复它？人们投票让我们上任。我们是人民的代表。选民会有最终决定权。下个月选票上就会有这个，所以人们将不得不决定哪种故事对他们来说更有意义。或者也许他们只会听到其中一个故事。这会让决定更容易。坦率地说，我无法想象大多数选民甚至知道JQC是什么。

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:40.980
<v Dave Kestenbaum>This referendum is not an example of democracy at its finest, though it's probably a pretty good example of how democracy actually is.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>这次公投不是民主处于最佳状态的一个例子，尽管它可能是一个相当好的例子，关于民主实际上是什么样的。

00:44:40.980 --> 00:45:00.290
<v Ira Glass>Dave Kestenbaum is one of the producers of our program. Coming up, one of the stars of Hamilton sings the words that Barack Obama is not saying but might be thinking. We are not sure if he's thinking this. We made this up. That's a minute, from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>戴夫·凯斯滕鲍姆是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，汉密尔顿的一位明星将唱出巴拉克·奥巴马没有说出但可能在想的话。我们不确定他是否在想这个。我们编造的。那是来自芝加哥公共广播电台的一分钟，我们的节目继续。

00:45:00.290 --> 00:45:14.250
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, the sometimes-frightening chasm between the way the two sides in this election see everything. We take stock. We have arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three, Aw, Do we NAFTA?
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，选举中两方对一切看法之间有时令人恐惧的鸿沟。我们进行评估。我们已经到达节目的第三幕。第三幕，啊，我们要做NAFTA吗？

00:45:14.250 --> 00:45:27.610
<v Ira Glass>So the one big thing this year that it has felt like the presidential candidates are not disagreeing about is free trade. But it's weird. Both parties used to agree free trade is good. This year, they seem to be agreeing it's bad.
<v Ira Glass>所以今年一件感觉总统候选人没有分歧的大事是自由贸易。但很奇怪。两党过去都同意自由贸易是好的。今年，他们似乎同意它是坏的。

00:45:27.610 --> 00:45:52.770
<v Ira Glass>During the primaries this year, when Bernie Sanders, Donald Trump, and Hillary Clinton were out bad-mouthing deals like the TPP and free trade in general, I had this feeling, like, wait, is it supposed to be good or bad? Like, I can't even tell anymore. Like, what happened? I felt genuinely confused about what to think. Are these deals good or bad? And so I asked Jacob Goldstein from NPR's economics podcast Planet Money to come in and just help me briefly weigh the pros and cons of these deals.
<v Ira Glass>今年初选期间，当伯尼·桑德斯、唐纳德·特朗普和希拉里·克林顿都在诋毁TPP和一般的自由贸易协议时，我有一种感觉，就像，等等，它到底是好是坏？就像，我甚至分不清了。就像，发生了什么？我对该想什么感到真正的困惑。这些协议是好是坏？所以我让NPR经济播客《Planet Money》的雅各布·戈尔茨坦进来，帮我简要权衡一下这些协议的利弊。

00:45:52.770 --> 00:46:00.420
<v Jacob Goldstein>I'm here. I talked to a lot of economists. I woke up in the middle of the night thinking about economic models of trade deals.
<v Jacob Goldstein>我来了。我和很多经济学家谈过。我半夜醒来思考贸易协议的经济模型。

00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:04.620
<v Ira Glass>I like that I asked you to come in, and you heeded the call, and then you lost sleep over it.
<v Ira Glass>我很高兴我让你进来，你响应了号召，然后你为此失去了睡眠。

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:06.410
<v Jacob Goldstein>I-- it's true. It's all true.
<v Jacob Goldstein>我——这是真的。这一切都是真的。

00:46:06.410 --> 00:46:21.780
<v Ira Glass>That's so great and sad at the same time. So, all right. Let's start with the obvious downside of these trade deals. We lose jobs, as a country. And the number that we hear when it comes to NAFTA is 700,000 jobs we've lost.
<v Ira Glass>这既伟大又悲伤。所以，好吧。让我们从这些贸易协议明显的缺点开始。我们作为一个国家失去了工作。关于NAFTA我们听到的数字是70万个工作岗位损失了。

00:46:21.780 --> 00:46:26.940
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah. I mean, 700,000 people is clearly a lot of people, right? It's football stadiums full of people.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。我的意思是，70万人显然是很多人，对吧？那是足球场挤满了人。

00:46:26.940 --> 00:46:27.330
<v Ira Glass>Yes.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:40.300
<v Jacob Goldstein>And if you are one of those people, NAFTA was really bad for you. So that's certainly true, and that's real. If you zoom out a little bit-- I mean, one thing. So that number-- that 700,000 number is over about 15 years.
<v Jacob Goldstein>如果你是那些人之一，NAFTA对你来说真的很糟糕。所以这当然是真的，那是真实的。如果你稍微拉远一点——我的意思是一件事。所以那个数字——那个70万的数字大约是15年来的。

00:46:40.300 --> 00:46:40.750
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:46:40.750 --> 00:46:55.690
<v Jacob Goldstein>So that's 700,000 jobs over 15 years. Just for context, the economy-- like, say, this year, is adding about 200,000 jobs a month. 200,000 jobs a month. So already this year, the US has added more than 700,000 jobs.
<v Jacob Goldstein>所以那是15年内70万个工作岗位。为了背景，经济——比如，今年，每个月增加大约20万个工作岗位。每个月20万个工作岗位。所以今年，美国已经增加了超过70万个工作岗位。

00:46:55.690 --> 00:47:00.160
<v Ira Glass>So wait, wait, wait. So I just want to be sure I've got these numbers right. So we're adding, like, 200,000 jobs a month.
<v Ira Glass>所以等等，等等，等等。所以我只是想确保我得到了这些数字。所以我们每个月增加大约20万个工作岗位。

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:02.610
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah, a little less than that. But about that, yeah.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的，稍微少一点。但大约是那样，是的。

00:47:02.610 --> 00:47:05.420
<v Ira Glass>And this is, like, a crazy boom time and that's super unusual?
<v Ira Glass>这像是一个疯狂的繁荣时期，那是超乎寻常的？

00:47:05.420 --> 00:47:10.690
<v Jacob Goldstein>I mean, it's a little high. But, like, normal would be, like, I don't, 150,000. Something like that. 130,000.
<v Jacob Goldstein>我的意思是，它有点高。但是，就像，正常情况会是，我不确定，15万。类似那样。13万。

00:47:10.690 --> 00:47:16.070
<v Ira Glass>So we're adding 200,000 a month right now, and this is 700,000 over 15 years.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们现在每个月增加20万，而这是15年来的70万。

00:47:16.070 --> 00:47:29.520
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yes. And, I mean, to be clear, it doesn't mean that there are 700,000 fewer jobs in America than there would have been. It's not like a hole, right? These jobs went away and other new jobs were created.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。而且，我的意思是，很清楚，这并不意味着美国的工作岗位比本来应该有的少了70万。它不像一个洞，对吧？这些工作消失了，其他新的工作被创造了出来。

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:45.950
<v Ira Glass>Right. But the thing that we always hear-- and Donald Trump has said this many times-- is that the good jobs are gone. Like, the good high-paying factory jobs, those are gone. Making cars in Detroit-- that's gone. And what we have instead are these kind of lousy lower-wage jobs.
<v Ira Glass>对。但我们一直听到的——唐纳德·特朗普多次说过——是好工作没了。就像，好的高薪工厂工作，那些没了。在底特律制造汽车——没了。而我们拥有的却是那种糟糕的低工资工作。

00:47:45.950 --> 00:48:06.690
<v Jacob Goldstein>That's true, and it's mostly not because of NAFTA. I mean, there are-- millions of factory jobs have gone away and, that's because of automation. Jobs for people with low skills are lower-paid partly because unions are weaker, so there are a lot of reasons it's been a really bad time to be a low-skilled worker in this country. Mostly not NAFTA.
<v Jacob Goldstein>那是真的，而且主要不是因为NAFTA。我的意思是，有——数百万工厂工作岗位消失了，那是因为自动化。低技能工人的工作工资更低部分原因是工会更弱，所以有很多原因导致这个国家对于低技能工人来说是一段非常糟糕的时期。大多不是NAFTA。

00:48:06.690 --> 00:48:11.460
<v Ira Glass>So in other words, don't blame Hillary Clinton and NAFTA, blame the robots.
<v Ira Glass>所以换句话说，别怪希拉里·克林顿和NAFTA，怪机器人。

00:48:11.460 --> 00:48:12.620
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yes.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。

00:48:12.620 --> 00:48:16.710
<v Ira Glass>OK. So that's the downside of NAFTA. And what's the positive side to NAFTA?
<v Ira Glass>好的。所以那是NAFTA的缺点。NAFTA的积极一面是什么？

00:48:16.710 --> 00:48:17.570
<v Jacob Goldstein>So there's--
<v Jacob Goldstein>所以有——

00:48:17.570 --> 00:48:19.220
<v Ira Glass>'Cause we are not hearing much about that.
<v Ira Glass>因为我们没有听到太多关于那个的消息。

00:48:19.220 --> 00:48:34.650
<v Jacob Goldstein>No, it's true. So there's two things. I mean, this is true of trade deals in general. There's two big upsides. And one of them, when politicians are selling trade deals, they talk about, and that is, we get to sell more stuff to other countries, right? They lower their tariffs, we get to sell more stuff to them.
<v Jacob Goldstein>不，这是真的。所以有两件事。我的意思是，这对一般贸易协议都适用。有两个巨大的优点。其中一个，当政客们在推销贸易协议时，他们谈论它，那就是，我们可以卖更多东西给其他国家，对吧？他们降低关税，我们能卖更多东西给他们。

00:48:34.650 --> 00:48:36.150
<v Ira Glass>And has that worked out?
<v Ira Glass>那奏效了吗？

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:38.780
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah. Yeah, we sell a lot more stuff to Mexico than we used to.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。是的，我们卖给墨西哥的东西比以前多得多。

00:48:38.780 --> 00:48:39.680
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:53.380
<v Jacob Goldstein>The other one-- the other one politicians almost never talk about, but it's huge in economics. It, like, goes all the way back to Adam Smith. It is the central idea. And that is, trade deals mean we get to buy stuff for cheaper.
<v Jacob Goldstein>另一个——另一个政客们几乎从不谈论，但它在经济学中是巨大的。它，就像，一直追溯到亚当·斯密。它是核心思想。那就是，贸易协议意味着我们能买到更便宜的东西。

00:48:53.380 --> 00:48:59.790
<v Ira Glass>In other words, people in Mexico and China are making stuff with their lower wages, and then they sell it to us and it's cheaper to us.
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，墨西哥和中国的人用他们更低的工资制造东西，然后卖给我们，这对我们来说更便宜。

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:00.700
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yes.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。

00:49:00.700 --> 00:49:14.400
<v Ira Glass>And so we live a little better because of that. And has anybody tried to, like, actually name the dollar amount that each of us makes on this? Like, how much do each of us make on NAFTA or the TPP?
<v Ira Glass>所以我们因为那个生活得稍微好了一点。有人试过，像，实际上命名我们每个人从中赚取的美元金额吗？就像，我们每个人从NAFTA或TPP中赚了多少？

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:34.640
<v Jacob Goldstein>So those numbers are hard, because there's lots of different things going on in the economy, and there's definitely ranges of estimates. But for NAFTA, one study I found by a guy at Yale that seems, like, very reasonable-- it uses pretty standard assumptions-- he found that in the US every year, we get an extra $15 billion because of NAFTA.
<v Jacob Goldstein>所以那些数字很难，因为经济中发生了很多不同的事情，并且绝对有估计的范围。但对于NAFTA，我在耶鲁大学发现的一项研究似乎，像，非常合理——它使用相当标准的假设——他发现美国每年，因为NAFTA我们额外获得了150亿美元。

00:49:34.640 --> 00:49:36.250
<v Ira Glass>That's $15 billion, with a B.
<v Ira Glass>那是150亿美元，带B。

00:49:36.250 --> 00:49:37.370
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。

00:49:37.370 --> 00:49:38.200
<v Ira Glass>Every year.
<v Ira Glass>每年。

00:49:38.200 --> 00:49:38.640
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yes.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。

00:49:38.640 --> 00:49:45.450
<v Ira Glass>Wow. And that's a combination of us exporting more stuff, and also we all save money on stuff that we import.
<v Ira Glass>哇。那是我们出口更多东西的组合，而且我们也都在进口东西上省钱。

00:49:45.450 --> 00:49:46.840
<v Jacob Goldstein>Exactly.
<v Jacob Goldstein>正是。

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:53.470
<v Ira Glass>So $15 billion a year is what the whole country makes. And when you divide that by the number of us in the country, how much is that?
<v Ira Glass>所以每年150亿美元是整个国家赚取的。当你把它除以国家里我们的人数，那是多少？

00:49:53.470 --> 00:50:12.320
<v Jacob Goldstein>So it's something like $100 per worker, per year. Which clearly is, like, pretty small, right? I mean, one of the striking things about NAFTA in general is that, when you look at the US economy as a whole, the economic effects of NAFTA are just not that big of a deal, plus or minus.
<v Jacob Goldstein>所以大约是每个工人每年100美元。这显然是，就像，相当小，对吧？我的意思是，NAFTA总的来说一个惊人的事情是，当你查看整个美国经济，NAFTA的经济影响加加减减真的不是那么大。

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:15.960
<v Ira Glass>That blows my mind too, to hear you say that, the way people talk about it.
<v Ira Glass>听到你这么说也让我大吃一惊，就像人们谈论它的方式。

00:50:15.960 --> 00:50:19.720
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah. I mean, I think a lot of trade is symbolism.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。我的意思是，我认为很多贸易是象征意义的。

00:50:19.720 --> 00:50:34.840
<v Ira Glass>And let's just go back for a second, OK? If it's 700,000 people lose their jobs, and then I only get $100 a year-- I don't know. $100? I think maybe I'd rather have somebody in Detroit keep their job. I got $100.
<v Ira Glass>让我们退一步，好吗？如果70万人失去工作，然后我每年只得到100美元——我不知道。100美元？我想也许我宁愿底特律的某个人保持他的工作。我得到了100美元。

00:50:34.840 --> 00:51:03.320
<v Jacob Goldstein>I mean, that is reasonable. You know, I think one of the reasons trade is so unpopular is, the pain is really concentrated and visible. And the benefits are really diffuse, right? Like, emotionally, like, who cares about $100? I mean, what economists would say is, in the long run-- in the long run, one trade deal after another, they add up, right? We all get significantly richer because of trade.
<v Jacob Goldstein>我的意思是，那是合理的。你知道，我认为贸易如此不受欢迎的原因之一是，痛苦真的很集中和明显。而好处真的很分散，对吧？就像，从情感上说，就像，谁在乎100美元？我的意思是，经济学家会说的是，长远来看——长远来看，一个接一个的贸易协议，它们加起来，对吧？我们所有人因为贸易变得显著更富有。

00:51:03.320 --> 00:51:14.040
<v Jacob Goldstein>In the case of a trade deal, there is this transition period where there is real pain for a lot of people. After the transition is over, we keep getting that extra money forever. And the transition is over.
<v Jacob Goldstein>在贸易协议的情况下，有一个过渡期，在那期间很多人有真正的痛苦。过渡结束后，我们继续永远得到那笔额外的钱。而过渡结束了。

00:51:14.040 --> 00:51:17.020
<v Ira Glass>OK. So overall, the economy is bigger.
<v Ira Glass>好的。所以总的来说，经济更大了。

00:51:17.020 --> 00:51:42.310
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yes. And that right there is why economists love free trade. It's one of the few things that almost all economists agree on. In fact, there's this survey that the University of Chicago did where they asked all these economists, all across the political spectrum, are Americans better off on average because of NAFTA? 95 percent said yes, 5% said they were unsure.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。而且就在那里，这就是为什么经济学家热爱自由贸易。这是几乎所有经济学家都同意的少数几件事之一。事实上，有一个芝加哥大学做的调查，他们问所有这些经济学家，跨越政治光谱，美国人因为NAFTA平均来说过得更好吗？95%说是，5%说不确定。

00:51:42.310 --> 00:51:43.490
<v Ira Glass>How many said no?
<v Ira Glass>多少人说不？

00:51:43.490 --> 00:51:44.700
<v Jacob Goldstein>None. Nobody.
<v Jacob Goldstein>没有。没人。

00:51:44.700 --> 00:51:45.870
<v Ira Glass>Nobody said no?
<v Ira Glass>没人说不？

00:51:45.870 --> 00:51:50.340
<v Jacob Goldstein>Not one economist said Americans are worse off on average because of NAFTA.
<v Jacob Goldstein>没有一个经济学家说美国人因为NAFTA平均来说过得更差。

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:53.940
<v Ira Glass>And when it comes to the TPP, the Trans-Pacific Partnership?
<v Ira Glass>当谈到TPP，跨太平洋伙伴关系时？

00:51:53.940 --> 00:51:55.250
<v Jacob Goldstein>Very good.
<v Jacob Goldstein>非常好。

00:51:55.250 --> 00:52:04.730
<v Ira Glass>So when we do estimates on how much we all might get if that were to go into effect-- like, how good are we going to do, each one of us, from that?
<v Ira Glass>所以当我们做估计我们所有人如果那生效可能会得到多少时——就像，我们要表现得有多好，我们每个人，从中？

00:52:04.730 --> 00:52:18.080
<v Jacob Goldstein>So the federal government estimate of that is, once it's fully phased in, will be about $60 billion, with a B, richer every year, we'll get that extra money. So if you do that per worker, it's like $375 per worker.
<v Jacob Goldstein>所以联邦政府对此的估计是，一旦它完全分阶段实施，每年将大约富裕600亿美元，带B，我们将得到那笔额外的钱。所以如果你按工人算，大约每个工人375美元。

00:52:18.080 --> 00:52:18.880
<v Ira Glass>Every year.
<v Ira Glass>每年。

00:52:18.880 --> 00:52:19.210
<v Jacob Goldstein>Every  year.
<v Jacob Goldstein>每年。

00:52:19.210 --> 00:52:22.640
<v Ira Glass>A little under $400 per worker gets that money.
<v Ira Glass>每个工人不到400美元得到那笔钱。

00:52:22.640 --> 00:52:58.350
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah. And, I mean, that's just dividing the total value by the number per worker. They're not saying everybody is going to make that much more money. And just to be clear, this is a super rough estimate. You know, nobody knows exactly what it's going to be. And I should point out one more thing about the TPP, and that is, a lot of the opposition to the TPP is not about what we've been talking about. It's not about reducing tariffs. It's about all this other stuff, like rules about patent protection, or what happens if a company doesn't like a regulation in a foreign country. TPP gives them ways to challenge that regulation. Some people don't like that.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。而且，我的意思是，那只是把总价值除以每个工人的数量。他们不是说每个人都会赚那么多钱。而且只是要清楚，这是一个超级粗略的估计。你知道，没有人确切知道那会是多少。而且我应该指出关于TPP的另一件事，那就是，很多对TPP的反对不是关于我们一直在谈论的东西。它不是关于减少关税。它关于所有这些其他东西，比如关于专利保护的规则，或者如果一家公司不喜欢外国的某种规定会发生什么。TPP给他们挑战该规定的方式。有些人不喜欢那样。

00:52:58.350 --> 00:53:06.170
<v Ira Glass>But the big picture with these trade deals is, like, a small number of workers get bonked on the head, and then the rest of us get a bigger economy.
<v Ira Glass>但这些贸易协议的大图景是，就像，少数工人被敲了脑袋，然后我们其余人得到一个更大的经济。

00:53:06.170 --> 00:53:11.200
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yes, although it is leaving out another piece, which is the other countries.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的，尽管它遗漏了另一块，也就是其他国家。

00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:17.470
<v Ira Glass>Jacob, I have two words to say to you. America first. Like, really? You want me to care about the people in the other countries?
<v Ira Glass>雅各布，我有两个词要对你说。美国优先。就像，真的吗？你想让我关心其他国家的人？

00:53:17.470 --> 00:53:48.010
<v Jacob Goldstein>You don't have to care about them. But, I mean, it's a fact that when the US trades more with people in poor countries, they get richer. And that is, for them-- maybe not for you, but for them-- a big deal. China is, like, the big case here, right? In the last 20 years, say, hundreds of millions of people-- like a whole America of people have come up out of, like, grinding, you know, subsistence farming, $2-a-day poverty basically because of trade with rich countries.
<v Jacob Goldstein>你不需要关心他们。但是，我的意思是，这是一个事实，当美国与贫穷国家的人贸易更多时，他们变得更富有。而且这对他们来说是——也许对你不重要，但对他们——一件大事。中国是，就像，大案例，对吧？在过去20年里，说，数亿人——就像整个美国的人已经从，就像，艰苦的，你知道，生存农业，2美元一天的贫困中基本因为与富国贸易而走出来。

00:53:48.010 --> 00:53:53.340
<v Jacob Goldstein>And clearly there's pollution because of that. Clearly those are terrible factory jobs.
<v Jacob Goldstein>而且显然因为那个有污染。显然那些是可怕的工厂工作。

00:53:53.340 --> 00:53:53.660
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:53:53.660 --> 00:54:08.080
<v Jacob Goldstein>But it has been an incredible anti-poverty program for China. There's this one example from Vietnam that's, like, really clear. What happened with Vietnam was, in 2001, the trade barriers with the US came down, like all of a sudden. Not all the way down, but--
<v Jacob Goldstein>但这对中国来说是一个令人难以置信的脱贫计划。有一个来自越南的例子，是，就像，真的很清楚。越南发生了什么是，2001年，与美国的贸易壁垒降下来了，就像突然间。不是完全降下来，但——

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:08.860
<v Ira Glass>Tariffs were dropped.
<v Ira Glass>关税被削减了。

00:54:08.860 --> 00:54:10.190
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah. They got a lot lower.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。它们低了很多。

00:54:10.190 --> 00:54:10.600
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:54:10.600 --> 00:54:19.300
<v Jacob Goldstein>And this economist in Canada realized like, oh, here is this natural experiment where we can just see-- like, there's a very clear before and after. What happens?
<v Jacob Goldstein>而且加拿大的一位经济学家意识到，就像，哦，这里有一个自然的实验，我们可以看到——就像，有一个非常清楚的前后。发生了什么？

00:54:19.300 --> 00:54:21.130
<v Ira Glass>Right. There wasn't free trade, now there is free trade.
<v Ira Glass>对。之前没有自由贸易，现在有自由贸易了。

00:54:21.130 --> 00:54:22.260
<v Jacob Goldstein>Freer. Much freer.
<v Jacob Goldstein>更自由。自由得多。

00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:23.040
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:54:23.040 --> 00:54:34.190
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah. And what he found-- so he looked at what happened in Vietnam over just the few years after this change happened. What he found was, in three years there were a quarter of a million factory jobs created.
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的。而且他发现——所以他查看了这种变化发生后仅仅几年内越南发生了什么。他发现的是，在三年内有25万个工厂工作岗位被创造了出来。

00:54:34.190 --> 00:54:35.530
<v Ira Glass>These are clothing?
<v Ira Glass>这些是服装？

00:54:35.530 --> 00:54:53.410
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah, like textiles. Like, it's low-end manufacturing, right? Yes. Like, cheap stuff. You know, what we would think of as very bad jobs, right? So there was this quarter of a million factory jobs. And then around those jobs this whole sort of economy grew up, these people at the factory jobs were now richer. They could buy more stuff, so--
<v Jacob Goldstein>是的，像纺织品。就像，它是低端制造业，对吧？是的。就像，便宜的东西。你知道，我们会认为是糟糕的工作，对吧？所以有这25万个工厂工作岗位。然后围绕那些工作这种整个经济成长起来，那些在工厂工作的人现在更富有了。他们可以买更多东西，所以——

00:54:53.410 --> 00:54:56.090
<v Ira Glass>Oh, people selling them food and apartments and all that sort of stuff.
<v Ira Glass>哦，人们卖给他们食物和公寓所有那类东西。

00:54:56.090 --> 00:55:14.190
<v Jacob Goldstein>Exactly. Exactly. And so you have millions of people coming out of poverty in three years. In three years, 7 million people come out of poverty in Vietnam. The poverty rate for the country falls by a third. And to be clear, that is not all because of this one trade deal with the US, but it is a big chunk of it.
<v Jacob Goldstein>正是。正是。所以你有数百万人三年内脱贫。在三年内，越南有700万人脱贫。该国的贫困率下降了三分之一。而且要清楚，那不全是这个与美国的一个贸易协议的功劳，但它占了很大一部分。

00:55:14.190 --> 00:55:32.690
<v Ira Glass>I mean, when you say that, the thing I picture is like, oh-- it's almost like there's, like, a certain amount of misery in the world, and then when we lower the trade barriers we import a little misery to the United States and a certain number of people lose their jobs, and we export a certain amount of our prosperity, and they get to have the prosperity.
<v Ira Glass>我的意思是，当你这么说时，我想象的东西是，就像，哦——几乎就像世界上有，就像，一定数量的痛苦，然后当我们降低贸易壁垒时我们进口了一点点痛苦到美国，一定数量的人失去了工作，我们出口了一定数量的我们的繁荣，而他们能够拥有那繁荣。

00:55:32.690 --> 00:55:49.060
<v Jacob Goldstein>The sort of classical economics answer to that is, in fact, when you have freer trade, the total amount of misery in the world declines. Yes, there are some people who get screwed, but on the whole the US gets richer and Vietnam gets richer. It's not zero sum.
<v Jacob Goldstein>那种古典经济学的答案是，事实上，当你拥有更自由的贸易时，世界上的痛苦总量下降了。是的，有一些人被搞砸了，但总的来说美国变得更富有，越南变得更富有。这不是零和游戏。

00:55:49.060 --> 00:55:51.060
<v Ira Glass>Right, but some individuals get screwed.
<v Ira Glass>对，但一些个人被搞砸了。

00:55:51.060 --> 00:55:54.380
<v Jacob Goldstein>Absolutely. That is absolutely true.
<v Jacob Goldstein>绝对。那是绝对真的。

00:55:54.380 --> 00:56:00.240
<v Ira Glass>I thought you were going to say the idealistic argument is, like, world peace. Like, if we're trading with each other, we don't kill each other or something.
<v Ira Glass>我以为你会说理想主义的争论是，就像，世界和平。就像，如果我们彼此贸易，我们就不会杀死彼此或者某种东西。

00:56:00.240 --> 00:56:03.130
<v Jacob Goldstein>Also that one. Sure. World peace.
<v Jacob Goldstein>也那个。当然。世界和平。

00:56:03.130 --> 00:56:10.520
<v Ira Glass>Jacob Goldstein. You can download the great show he co-hosts, NPR's Planet Money podcast wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>雅各布·戈尔茨坦。你可以在任何你获取播客的地方下载他联合主持的伟大节目，NPR的Planet Money播客。

00:56:10.520 --> 00:56:14.170
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us to Act Four, Seriously?
<v Ira Glass>这把我们带到了第四幕，真的吗？

00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:26.420
<v Barack Obama>Facts, evidence, reason, logic, an understanding of science. These are good things.
<v Barack Obama>事实、证据、理性、逻辑、对科学的理解。这些都是好事。

00:56:26.420 --> 00:56:48.160
<v Ira Glass>Well, the sometimes-fact-free utterances of one of the candidates for president this year has been such an issue that several times Barack Obama has spoken about facts and their importance over the last few months. At the Rutgers University commencement this spring he talked about how we live in a moment when, quote, "opinions masquerade as facts, and the wildest conspiracy theories are taken as gospel."
<v Ira Glass>嗯，今年其中一位总统候选人有时脱离事实的言论已经成为一个问题，以至于过去几个月巴拉克·奥巴马多次谈到事实及其重要性。在今年春天的罗格斯大学毕业典礼上，他谈到了我们如何生活在一个时刻，引用，“观点伪装成事实，而最狂野的阴谋论被视为真理。”

00:56:48.160 --> 00:57:41.470
<v Barack Obama>When our leaders express a disdain for facts, when they're not held accountable for repeating falsehoods and just making stuff up, while actual experts are dismissed as elitists, then we've got a problem. Look, our nation's founders-- Franklin, Madison, Hamilton, Jefferson-- they were born of the Enlightenment. They sought to escape superstition and sectarianism and tribalism and know-nothing-ness. They believed in rational thought, and experimentation, and the capacity of informed citizens to master our own fates. That is embedded in our constitutional design.
<v Barack Obama>当我们的领导人表达对事实的蔑视，当他们因重复虚假信息和编造东西而不被追究责任，而真正的专家被斥为精英主义者时，我们就有问题了。看，我们国家的缔造者——富兰克林、麦迪逊、汉密尔顿、杰斐逊——他们生于启蒙运动。他们寻求逃离迷信、教派主义、部落主义和无知。他们相信理性思考、实验，以及知情公民掌握我们自己命运的能力。那是嵌入在我们宪法设计中的。

00:57:41.470 --> 00:58:06.760
<v Ira Glass>There's this thing that we've been talking about here at our program, that Donald Trump probably would not have happened if there had not been a black president. You know, like, Trump rose to prominence among Republicans as the leading proponent of the idea that Barack Obama was not born in the United States. And the sudden visibility that we're seeing of white nationalism during this presidential race-- that is hard to imagine if there had not been a black president right before it.
<v Ira Glass>我们在这里的节目中一直在谈论这件事，如果没有一位黑人总统，唐纳德·特朗普可能不会发生。你知道，就像，特朗普在共和党人中崛起为巴拉克·奥巴马不是出生在美国这一观点的领先倡导者。而且我们在这次总统竞选中看到的白人民族主义突然可见度——如果之前没有一位黑人总统，那是很难想象的。

00:58:06.760 --> 00:58:32.440
<v Ira Glass>Donald Trump is one of the consequences of the Obama presidency. And we've been talking about how that's probably a thought that's occurred to the president. But, as far as we can tell, he has not spoken about that publicly. And so wondering what his thoughts might be, we decided to try something. We asked a songwriter, Sara Bareilles, to write a song imagining Barack Obama's thoughts on that and on Trump, and we present that now.
<v Ira Glass>唐纳德·特朗普是奥巴马总统任期的后果之一。我们一直在谈论这可能是一个总统已经想到的想法。但是，据我们所知，他还没有公开谈论过这一点。所以好奇他的想法可能是什么，我们决定尝试一些东西。我们请了一位词曲作者，萨拉·巴雷里斯，写一首歌，想象巴拉克·奥巴马对那件事以及对特朗普的想法，我们现在展示它。

00:58:32.440 --> 00:58:43.490
<v Ira Glass>Sarah puts out albums of her own, but she also wrote a Broadway show, Waitress , and she invited a Broadway colleague to sing this, Leslie Odom Jr., one of the stars of Hamilton . Here's the song.
<v Ira Glass>萨拉发行自己的专辑，但她也写了一部百老汇剧，Waitress，她邀请了一位百老汇同事来唱这个，小莱斯利·奥多姆，汉密尔顿的一位明星。这就是这首歌。

00:58:43.490 --> 00:58:52.690
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - SARA BAREILLES, "SERIOUSLY"]
<v Ira Glass>（音乐 - 萨拉·巴雷里斯，“SERIOUSLY”）

00:58:52.690 --> 00:59:10.040
<v Leslie Odom Jr>(SINGING) Let's start with hope. I threw it in the mirror like a skipping stone, the ripples won, son of a gun. Some would not have thought so, but I stand here, Commander-in-Chief.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>（演唱）让我们从希望开始。我把它像一颗跳跃的石头扔进镜子里，涟漪赢了，孩子，枪的儿子。有些人不会这么想，但我站在这里，总司令。

00:59:10.040 --> 00:59:15.220
<v Leslie Odom Jr>And I take that seriously.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>我严肃对待这一点。

00:59:15.220 --> 00:59:38.780
<v Leslie Odom Jr>But along the way, a rogue ripple turned tidal wave in reaction to what I tried to do, the rebirth of a Nation's hatred, red, white, and blue. Is black in there, too? Seriously.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>但一路走来，一个流氓涟漪变成了潮汐波，作为对我尝试所做之事的反应，国家仇恨的重生，红、白、蓝。黑色也在里面吗？真的吗。

00:59:38.780 --> 01:00:02.830
<v Leslie Odom Jr>One man rewriting the book on bad behavior. Maybe cheats the neighbors. Feels he gets what they paid for. We can't pat him on the back and send him on through. No man's ignorance will ever be his virtue.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>一个人正在重写关于不良行为的书。也许欺骗邻居。感觉他得到了他们支付的东西。我们不能拍拍他的背让他过去。没有人的无知会永远是他的美德。

01:00:02.830 --> 01:00:14.170
<v Leslie Odom Jr>Is this the best we can be? Seriously.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>这是我们能做到的最好吗？真的吗。

01:00:14.170 --> 01:00:35.970
<v Leslie Odom Jr>Let's talk of fear and why I don't bring it in here. It's a dangerous word, spooks the herd, and we all bleed in the stampede. Fear makes a false friend indeed. And I take it seriously.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>让我们谈谈恐惧以及为什么我不把它带到这里。这是一个危险的词，惊吓了兽群，我们都在奔涌中流血。恐惧确实制造了一个虚假的朋友。我严肃对待它。

01:00:35.970 --> 01:00:54.380
<v Leslie Odom Jr>Oh, hear me now, for the truth gets drowned out by a demigod flexing. A demigod flexing. He's history repeating.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>哦，现在听我说，因为真理被一个半神在炫耀中淹没了。一个半神在炫耀。他是历史的重复。

01:00:54.380 --> 01:00:58.370
<v Leslie Odom Jr>Angry.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>愤怒。

01:00:58.370 --> 01:01:03.350
<v Leslie Odom Jr>Am I angry?
<v Leslie Odom Jr>我愤怒吗？

01:01:03.350 --> 01:01:09.820
<v Leslie Odom Jr>You ask, am I angry?
<v Leslie Odom Jr>你问，我愤怒吗？

01:01:09.820 --> 01:01:41.260
<v Leslie Odom Jr>And I'm at a loss for words. After all we've done, every battle hard won, every hair gone gray in the name of this place, in a history plagued with incredible mistakes. Still, I pledge my allegiance to these united, divided states.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>而我失去了语言。在我们所做的一切之后，每一场战斗艰苦赢得，每一根头发因这个地方的名字变灰，在一段充满难以置信的错误的历史中。仍然，我誓言效忠于这些联合的、分裂的州。

01:01:41.260 --> 01:01:52.220
<v Leslie Odom Jr>Seriously.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>真的吗。

01:01:52.220 --> 01:02:09.150
<v Leslie Odom Jr>Let's end with, why? It's a question I want to ask of us as a populace. Why not take our time for the weight of this story?
<v Leslie Odom Jr>让我们以为什么结束？这是我想问我们作为一个民众的问题。为什么不花时间去了解这个故事的重量？

01:02:09.150 --> 01:02:16.710
<v Leslie Odom Jr>Seriously.
<v Leslie Odom Jr>真的吗。

01:02:16.710 --> 01:02:25.220
<v Ira Glass>Leslie Odom Jr., singing words and music by Sarah Bareilles. We also shot a video of Leslie Odom Jr. singing this song. It'll be up online soon.
<v Ira Glass>小莱斯利·奥多姆，演唱萨拉·巴雷里斯的词曲。我们也拍摄了一个小莱斯利·奥多姆演唱这首歌的视频。它很快会在网上发布。

01:02:25.220 --> 01:02:42.840
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Dana Chivvis and Susan Burton. Our production staff, Zoe Chace, Sean Cole, Neil Drumming, Karen Duffin, Emmanuel Dzotsi, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, David Kestenbaum, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Robyn Semien, Matt Tierney, and Nancy Updike. Research help today from Christopher Swetala and Michelle Harris. Music help from Damien Grave.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们的节目今天由达娜·奇维斯和苏珊·伯顿制作。我们的制作人员，佐伊·蔡斯、肖恩·科尔、尼尔·德拉明、凯伦·达芬、伊曼纽尔·佐齐、斯蒂芬妮·傅、查娜·乔夫-沃尔特、大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆、米基·米克、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔、罗宾·塞米恩、马特·蒂尔尼和南希·厄普代克。今天来自克里斯托弗·斯威塔拉和米歇尔·哈里斯的研究帮助。来自达米恩·格雷夫的音乐帮助。

01:02:42.840 --> 01:03:12.920
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>（致谢）

01:03:12.920 --> 01:03:28.100
<v Ira Glass>Our website, ThisAmericanLife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks as always to our program's co-founder Mr. Torey Malatia, who is so tired of dealing with radio hosts. They are so spoiled.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，ThisAmericanLife.org。《美国生活》由PRX，公共广播交易所，传递给公共广播电台。像往常一样感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生，他厌倦了与电台主持人打交道。他们太被宠坏了。

01:03:28.100 --> 01:03:34.780
<v Richard Hyde>People stand up when they walk in the room, they stand up when they leave, they bring them coffee, they laugh at their dumb jokes.
<v Richard Hyde>人们在他们走进房间时站起来，在他们离开时站起来，他们给他们拿咖啡，他们对他们的愚蠢笑话大笑。

01:03:34.780 --> 01:03:39.780
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。
